- Flat partial applications on the C++ backend (T7).  A partial
  application stored its arguments as a cons list: PartApplicNode held the
  missing count, the head, and a terms list; create_partial built one cons
  per argument, apply allocated a new PartApplic and a cons per added
  argument, and from_partial walked the list.  Done: the arguments are
  inline.  Slot 0 holds the missing count, slot 1 the info table of the
  head, and the slots after them the arguments in call order, so a partial
  application of n arguments has arity 2 + n and the format "ix" followed
  by n times 'p'.  Each n has an info table of its own: PartApplic_Info is
  the table without arguments, and partapplic_info(n) (PartialInfoFamily in
  builtins.hpp, constant-initialized, the first 32 tables by index and the
  rest by a map) makes the others on demand, with the name, flags, and type
  of the base; PartialS has a family of its own (partials_info).  So the
  collector, the copier, the equality, show, the walkers, and the bindings
  read a partial application by its format like any node.  create_partial
  is one allocation, from_partial and rewrite_from_partial copy the
  argument words, apply on an incomplete application allocates one node
  with one more argument (Node::extend_partial), applyS likewise, and set
  copies the arguments under the other family.  A partial application
  without arguments (a function used as a value, f in map f xs) is a node
  of the module, made once at load in the arena of the literal nodes
