- ICurry interpreter prototype in the C++ runtime (B2, issue #31).  Every
  module of the C++ backend went through g++ before it could run: a warm
  compile of an expression paid 0.23 s of g++, a module compiled from a
  string about 0.7 s, and the Python backend was the only evaluator that
  needed no compiler.  Done: the runtime interprets a function from a
  bytecode.  InfoTable gets a field aux, the data of the step (null for
  generated code and the built-ins).  cyrt/icurry.hpp defines the machine:
  registers (plain Node * for the arguments a step only passes on, the
  rule of backends/cxx/passthrough.py), variables (Variable objects for
  the scrutinees and the bases of paths), and an operand stack on which
  the arguments of a node are collected; 25 opcodes in 32-bit units (the
  loads and the indexer, copies, a fresh free variable, pushes, MAKE,
  MAKE_PARTIAL, MAKE_STRING, the stores, SET_SUCC for a recursive let,
  EXEMPT, RET_REF, RET_NODE, RET_STRING, CASE_CONS with a branch table by
  tag, CASE_LIT with a value table).  cyrt/icurry.cpp is the one step
  function of every interpreted function (icurry_step): it makes the calls
  of the generated code (Node::successor_node, the indexer of Variable,
  rts->hnf, Node::create, create_partial, and the in-place rewrite of the
  redex when the result fits) and returns the same statuses, so compiled
  and interpreted functions run on one scheduler, take the same steps, and
  allocate the same nodes.  backends/cxx/bytecode.py emits the bytecode
  from the ICurry of a function (compile_function), with a disassembler;
  the constants of a function are the info tables and data types it names
  and the values the runtime makes once (the literal nodes, a partial
  application without arguments, the text of a string, the value set of a
  literal case).  backends/cxx/materialize.py attaches the bytecode when
  it materializes a function of a module imported from its ICurry
  (icurry_attach in the bindings); a function of the module named before
  the loader reaches it gets its table on demand.  The flag interpret
  selects the mode: off (the default: g++ as before), new (a module
  without a compiled object is interpreted from its JSON, the plan ends
  there: Json2Cpp.ends_plan, Maker.done; a module with a current object is
  loaded from it), all (every module is interpreted, the Prelude from its
  JSON, the compiled objects unused).  A module whose functions are
  interpreted keeps its tables for the life of the process (Module::clear),
  because the bytecode of another module names them by pointer.  Found on
  the way: the IOError constructors and the show instance of IOError were
  looked up through the symbols of Prelude.so (dlsym), so a catch or an
  I/O error dumped core when the Prelude was interpreted; the lookup goes
  through the module registry now (Module::find_symbol), which serves both
  forms.  Measured on the C++ backend (CPU seconds of the whole process
  from sprite-exec --stats, median of three; the -t evaluation seconds in
  parentheses; steps and forks equal in every mode): compiled, Tak1 1.31
  (1.13), Fib 0.34 (0.17), PermSort 5.09 (4.89), QueensSet 0.80 (0.61),
  Last 0.44 (0.27), ColormapFree 0.30 (0.16); interpret:new (the program
  interpreted, the Prelude compiled): Tak1 1.87 (1.74), Fib 0.31 (0.18),
  PermSort 5.34 (5.21), QueensSet 0.76 (0.62), Last 0.41 (0.28),
  ColormapFree 0.32 (0.18); interpret:all (every module interpreted; the
  process reads the ICurry of the Prelude and emits its bytecode first,
  0.55 s: Hello 0.14 to 0.69 s): Tak1 2.43 (1.74), Fib 0.93 (0.25),
  PermSort 5.83 (5.11), QueensSet 1.39 (0.61), Last 0.99 (0.29),
  ColormapFree 0.90 (0.21).  So the interpreter costs at most 1.5 times
  the compiled evaluation time (Tak1), and at most 2.7 times the compiled
  process (Fib, where the fixed 0.55 s dominate); the acceptance asked for
  at most 4.  The Python backend takes Tak1 282.8, Fib 29.7, PermSort
  278.7, QueensSet 47.5, ColormapFree 16.5 s (Last fails there with status
  135), 18 to 116 times the interpreted process; the acceptance asked for
  8.  A warm compile of an expression to its
  first value (probe.py with the ICurry cache warm) takes 0.019 to 0.020 s
  under interpret:new against 0.23 to 0.25 s with g++; cold 0.43 against
  0.67 s.  The compiled path is unchanged (compare: 5 same, counters
  equal).  perf on Tak1 under interpret:all: icurry_step 32 percent, hnf 9,
  Variable::skip 9, procS 5.5, the Python emitter of the Prelude 15; 29.6 G
  instructions against 16.2 G compiled.  Tests: unit_cxx_interp.py with
  data/curry/CxxInterp.curry (the emitter on hand-built ICurry: the plain
  loads and the indexer, a case with and without every branch, literal
  cases on Int, Char, and Float with the 64-bit words, the literals and
  strings as constants, partial applications and choices, a free variable
  and a recursive let, aliases and paths, an external body, the errors,
  the disassembler, and the opcode table against the runtime; the values
  of 46 goals of the module under interpret:all against the compiled code,
  with every kind of statement and expression, free variables, narrowing,
  set functions, functional patterns, IO, and strings; the loader under
  new, all, and off; the refusals of icurry_attach; one step at the root of
  a raw expression for each shape of result; Peano, Fib, and Last in
  children in every mode with equal output and steps, and in the stress
  mode of the collector).  The unit suite passes with the flag off (59
  files); under interpret:all the functional suites setfunctions, kiel,
  residuation, complete, examples, funpat, readshow, and io pass, and so do
  the unit files of the Prelude, equality, expressions, inspection, IO
  binding, the collector, the stack guard, the scheduler, the heap, the
