2026-10-05: finishing the integration branch
- compile(mode='expr') returns a node again.  After the typed branch a text
  with a where-free clause gave a Goal object whose values were Bindings for
  every lifted variable, which TestInstantiation of unit_py_runtime.py and
  test_apply_nf of unit_prelude.py do not take: they build a free variable
  with `x :: T where x free`.  The rule now: a lifted variable whose type is
  absent from the result type of the expression cannot show in a value, so
  its binding is reported (goals.absent_from_result compares the parameter
  types of the scheme of compiled_expression with its result type); a
  variable whose type occurs in the result type is left in the value, which
  is the answer.  `xs ++ [3] =:= [1,2,3] where xs free` still gives
  {xs=[1, 2]} True; `x where x free` gives _a, `(x, y) where x, y free`
  gives (_a, _b), `head (x:xs) where x, xs free` gives {xs=_b} _a, and
  `x =:= 1 where x free` gives {x=1} True.  The names travel with the node:
  the expression module declares `data SpriteLiftedGoal a = SpriteLiftedGoal
  a`, the root is that constructor around the tuple of the expression and
  the reported variables, and curry.eval finds the names in a registry of
  the interpreter keyed by the info table of the constructor
  (goals.register_lifted and goals.lifted_goal; reset clears it).  A
  constructor node is never rewritten, so the root is recognized after an
  evaluation as before it; a first design keyed the registry by the call of
  compiled_expression, which the first evaluation rewrote in place.  A side
  table keyed by the node was not an option: the Python node has no weak
  reference slot and the C++ wrapper hashes by value.  The Goal objects
  stay inside curry.eval, the REPL and sprite-exec.  The derived Data
  instance of the new data type exposed the ordering defect behind the known
  failure of test_lambda_goal: the Python backend compiled an expression
  module function by function while the module loaded, and a reference to a
  function of the module loaded later (a lifted lambda, a derived method)
  failed in importSymbol.  An expression module now loads with lazy
  trampolines like any module and is compiled right after its load, before
  it leaves the registry (Backend.compile_pending); test_lambda_goal passes
  on both backends.  The lifted variable is a free marker whose first step
  makes the variable, so test_apply_nf steps four times.  PAKCS prints the
  binding of every lifted variable, {x=x} x, so func_goal_defaulting.py
  drops from the golden the bindings of the variables Sprite leaves in the
  value before the comparison (drop_bindings); the goldens are unchanged.
- The Goal object no longer roots the history of a search.  curry.eval
  dropped its Goal after the merges, but the REPL's :eval, sprite-exec and
  a saved module's default goal held the goal node in a local variable
  through the evaluation, and the history (the choices pulled to the root
  with their failed alternatives) stayed reachable from it: under
  SPRITE_GC_STRESS=1 every collection marked it, and the run grew with the
  square of the search.  Now Goal.evaluate hands the node to the runtime
  state and keeps no reference to it (a second evaluation of the same Goal
  is a ValueError; curry.eval makes a new Goal per call), the REPL passes
  the compiled expression straight to curry.eval, and sprite-exec builds
  the goal when the evaluation starts.  Measured through the REPL under the
  stress mode: last (replicate 3000 True) took 26.5 s before and 1.0 s
  after; 8000 elements 1.1 s after; Last through sprite-exec 2.5 s, as the
  merge entry measured with the Goal dropped in curry.eval.
  test_repl_eval_under_stress of unit_goals.py runs the REPL on 8000
  elements under the stress mode within 60 s, on the C++ backend.
