2026-10-05: the bug batch (issues #32, #34, #36, #38, #59, #61)
- Set functions over a decided argument (#61).  `let t free in (t =:= (A ?
  B)) &> [sortValues (set1 id t)]` gave [[A]] for both alternatives on both
  backends.  Cause: the application of the set function sits below the
  pull-tab of the constraint, so the alternatives share the node; the first
  to reach it ran evalS once and wrote `Values (allValues (SetEval s q))`
  into the shared graph.  The nested queue reads the fingerprint of the
  enclosing configuration through the queue stack (read_fp and fork walk
  qstack, rts_fingerprint.cpp), and choice_escapes (rts_setfunctions.cpp)
  keeps a choice inside the capsule when an enclosing configuration has
  made it, so the capsule pruned the generator of t to the side of the
  first alternative and cached A in the shared allValues node, which the
  second alternative read.  A choice the configuration decided before the
  set function saw it had the same defect, in the argument (`let x = A ? B
  in (x, sortValues (set1 id x))` gave (A, [A]) and (B, [A])) and in the
  function position (set1 (constT x) 0).  Fix: evalS_step
  (currylib/setfunctions.cpp) and evalS (backends/py/currylib/
  setfunctions.py) walk the PartialS node before the rewrite
  (holds_private_state, _holds_private_state): a free variable with a
  binding, a narrowing, or a group, or a choice the configuration decided
  (read_fp, through the queue stack), makes the step private, and the
  replacement goes into a private copy of the spine from the root
  (copy_spine and E_RESTART; rts.restart() on the Python backend), as
  replace_freevar does for a binding; the shared node stays an application
  for the other configurations, which make their own set and queue.  The
  walk follows every pointer successor (calls, forward nodes, guards,
  partial applications, data, the alternatives of an undecided choice),
  does not enter the generator of a free variable (the variable is the
  test: nothing below its generator is decided while it is not), and gives
  up after 64 nodes with the conservative answer, private, which only loses
  sharing.  When the outermost queue holds one configuration and no queue
  encloses it, no other configuration reads the shared graph and a later
  clone starts with the same state, so the step answers shared without a
  walk: a deterministic program never pays the walk and never loses the
  sharing of an application to the bound.  The second part of the issue,
  `inner $## t` and `inner $!! t` on the Python backend (EvaluationSuspended
  and a ValueError in the issue; a hang here): N (fairscheme.py) runs with
  var.root = the redex when a step normalizes its argument ($!! and $##
  through _applyspecial), but its T_FREE branches copied the spine from
  rts.E along a path relative to var.root, which wrote the generator into
  the wrong slot, and its non-root T_CHOICE branch pull-tabbed the start of
  the walk instead of the choice at the cursor.  Fixed: the three copy_spine
  calls take rts.C.realpath, as hnf does, and the pull-tab takes
  state.cursor along state.realpath.  Tests: unit_setfunctions_bugs.py with
  data/curry/SetFunctionsBugs.curry (TestBoundVariable: the example of the
  issue, two set functions, the nested set function on the C++ backend,
  $## and $!!, the decided choice in the argument and in the function
  position, the walk behind a call, a forward node, a partial application
  and a data structure, an 81-element argument beyond the bound with the
  variable last, an 80-element one without a variable in a deterministic
  program and under a decided choice).  PAKCS cannot check the goals here:
  the pinned installation has no Control.SetFunctions (the setfunctions
  package is not installed), and the goldens of func_setfunctions.py are
  committed.  Relation to the dissertation (chapter 4, Figure 4.2, rule
  SF.1): a choice in the escape set always pull-tabs out of the capsule,
  and the two capsules it produces hold the alternatives themselves; no
  rule reads the state of an enclosing configuration.  Sprite's Queue::split
  leaves an undecided configuration in both queues without resolving the
  choice, and compensates with the refusal in choice_escapes and the walk
  of fork and read_fp through the queue stack; that refusal is where a
  capsule stops being configuration-neutral while its values live in the
  shared graph.  So the defect is an implementation deviation, not a flaw
  in the mechanics, and the fix above is the runtime's own invariant (state
  private to a configuration goes into a private spine, never into the
  shared graph) applied one step earlier; it is exact at creation time
  only.  Left, as test_narrowed_after_start (an expected failure on both
  backends): a capsule that starts while the variable is unbound, is
  consumed in part, and then resumes under alternatives that narrowed the
  variable differently (`let vs = valuesOf (set2 pickOrCase A u) in (head
  vs =:= A) &> ((u =:= (A ? B)) &> vs)` gives [A, A] twice; correct: [A, A]
  and [A, B]).  The clean repair is at the escape: split resolves the
  choice in the clones of the undecided configurations (LEFT in the kept
  queue, RIGHT in the new one), choice_escapes escapes every choice of the
  escape set that the nested configuration itself has not decided, and the
  nested fork and read_fp no longer need the enclosing fingerprints for
  those choices; the capsule becomes configuration-neutral and the
  creation-time walk goes away.  It touches queue.cpp, rts_setfunctions.cpp,
  rts_fingerprint.cpp, and allValues and filter_queue of the Python
  backend, and needs the set-function corpus and QueensSet (a split for a
  choice the outside decided produces a choice node the enclosing fork
  prunes at once).  To decide first: a choice captured through the function
  position (test_captured_argument of unit_cxx_scheduler.py, choiceCaptured
  here) is in no escape set; today the nested fork prunes it by the
  enclosing fingerprint, which gives (A, [A]) and (B, [B]) for
  choiceCaptured, the call-time choice reading of the shared x.
