- Parallel prebuild of the Curry library (sprite-make --jobs).  sprite-make
  has a --jobs N option; auto is one job per processor.  It makes up to N
  modules at once.  Each module is made by a child process that runs
  sprite-make on that module alone.  The parent resolves the named modules
  and the closure of their imports, keeps the modules whose files are
  stale, and starts a child only when the children of its stale imports
  have ended.  A module whose files are current gets no child; it passes
  the jobs of its stale imports on to its importers.  The imports of a
  module come from its source (the lenient scan of curry.cache, with the
  implicit Prelude) or, without a source, from its JSON file; a cycle adds
  no edge.  The order is needed because the interpreter compiles a stale
  import on the spot when a module is imported, so two children would
  write the files of one module.  A failed child stops its importers ('X
  was not made because an import failed'); without -k no further child
  starts, and the running ones end by themselves.  With -o the one named
  module is made in the parent after its imports.  The output of a child
  is written when it ends, so the lines of one module stay together.
  Without --jobs, and with --jobs 1, the run is the serial loop as before.
  curry/Makefile passes the job count of make to the C++ step alone
  (PREBUILD_SO_FLAGS): PREBUILD_JOBS reads the -jN of MAKEFLAGS at recipe
  time (while the makefiles are read, MAKEFLAGS carries -j without the
  count), auto for a bare -j, else JOBS of Make.config, so make -C curry
  install follows the configured width too; JOBS=1 keeps the serial run.
  The ICurry and Python steps stay serial: 3.8 s and 1.5 s serial against
  4.4 s and 1.9 s with 12 children.  Measured with four other streams on
  the machine (1-minute load beside each number): the C++ step over the 13
  modules on a scratch copy of the installation, alternating, serial 56.0
  and 43.0 s against 31.0 and 33.8 s with 12 jobs (load 4 to 6); single
  samples 37.7 against 26.0 s.  Prelude.so is one translation unit of 28.4
  s (with the precompiled header) and bounds the step; the other twelve
  modules take 0.5 to 2.6 s each.  Full builds in place: clean 44.7 s
  before (load 3.4 to 3.9) against 39.0 s after (load 3.5 to 4.5), with
  48.5 s (load up to 7.0) and 60.9 s (load 6.4) under heavier load; a
  header change 36.9 and 32.8 s before against 31.9 and 31.7 s after (load
  4 to 5), 39.9 and 37.7 s at load 9; a no-op stage and a touch of
  node.hpp 6.1 to 6.4 s before and after.  The trees of the two codes
  differ in the member name of the precompiled header, libcyrt.a (archive
  order) and libcyrt.so alone.  Tests: TestJobs in unit_make.py (option,
  child command, import scan, graph with a current import and a cycle,
  imports first with the children recorded, width, serial path, failure
  with and without -k, -o, the bytecode of a current module) and
  test_prebuild_recipe_follows_the_job_count in unit_prebuild.py.  Next:
  Cpp2So compiles with -c and links separately, so ccache hits the modules
  and the Prelude compile overlaps the runtime build; the code generator
  could split the Prelude into several units; the children of sprite-make
  are outside the job server of make; the prepare pass of the test runner
  could pass --jobs; CompilationPipeline.rst could mention the option.
- The runner follow-ups.  Section 10 of tests/README was edited in place.
  The rules decided: (1) Fast tier: --fast keeps the files below S seconds
  on every backend of the run and the files without an entry, minus the
  functional tests and the other files that compile a corpus of their own
  (the files CORPUS in lib/testrunner/prepare.py names: unit_benchmarks.py,
  unit_cxx_variable.py; selection.compiles_corpus).  The manifest measures
  a warm tree; on a cold tree such a file first compiles its corpus (the
  math corpus about 90 s on the C++ backend against 3 s warm).  Run them
  by name: run_tests 'func_*.py'.  Tier sizes before and after: cxx 71 to
  52 files, py 74 to 55, both 69 to 50.  (2) Cap: twice the manifest peak,
  at least 1 GB (MIN_CAP; covers the toolchain inside the test process on
  a cold tree; before: 512 MB).  While a process of the Curry toolchain
  runs in the session (TOOLS in procs.py: the front end, icurry or PAKCS
  on swipl, the C++ compiler and linker) the cap is at least 2 GB
  (TOOL_CAP); icurry on swipl alone takes about 1 GB (unit_curry2icurry.py:
  131 MB with a warm ICurry cache, 1042 MB cold).  The line of the file
  notes the allowance; the manifest records the peak.  The budget counts
  the manifest caps, not the allowance; the free 40 percent of
  MemAvailable absorbs the compilers of a cold tree.  (3) Prepare pass:
  advisory.  sprite-make runs with -k; a directory whose module failed
  ends 'incomplete', not 'FAILED'; its line names the modules without a
  product (the .py or .so under the directory sprite-make -S names) and
  its log; one summary line follows the pass ('prepare: 37 modules in 1
  directory on cxx, every product present'); the last line of the table
  adds 'prepare: N of M incomplete'; the exit status ignores the pass.
  (4) perf: a shell between perf and the command reports a signal death as
  128 plus the signal, which the harness maps back; nothing is parsed from
  perf's text (measure.py docstring).  (5) test_committed_manifest_loads
  compares the shape of the manifest, not its numbers.  A cold copy of the
  tree ran the fast tier of 52 files on cxx in 112.9 s at -j 2 --mem 4
  (load 5 to 6).
- The review of the three items, applied.  (1) Under the interpreter flag
  interpret set to new or all, sprite-make --so ends at the JSON
