2026-10-05: the primitives' names and the symbol table of a compiled module

- After the merge of stage 2, func_io's goal `error` failed on the C++
  backend under the tiered default with "bad Curry string", on the tree
  before the merge as well.  Cause: Module::link keys the symbol table of a
  compiled module by the name of each info table, and fifteen primitives
  behind externals carried the name of the Curry function that wraps them
  (prim_error was shown as "error", prim_showIntLiteral as
  "showIntLiteral", and so on).  So `Prelude.error`, looked up by name,
  resolved to the primitive without the `$##` of the Curry function, and an
  unevaluated (++) reached extract_string.  Only a caller that runs
  interpreted meets it: compiled code links the function directly, and
  under interpret:all the Prelude's tables are built from its ICurry with
  the Curry names.  The lookup by name also serves Python-built
  expressions, so curry.eval(P.error, <unevaluated message>) failed the
  same way.  Fix: every prim_X table is named prim_X (its Curry name; the
  name shows in a partial application and in traces, where PAKCS shows the
  Curry name too); Module::link asserts that a name maps to one table; and
  extract_string follows forward nodes, as Node::successor_node does, and
  names the offending node in its error.  Tests: unit_prelude.py
  TestErrorMessage (a module compiled from text raises the full message on
  both backends; the primitives' names on the C++ backend); func_io passes
  with the io corpus interpreted first; unit_cxx_tiered's
  test_nondet_io_argument_error, the mixed-mode gap of the B3 entry above
  (an interpreted module applying readFile of the compiled Prelude), passes
  and lost its expected-failure mark: it was the same defect.
- Two tests adjusted to the merged tree: func_typed_expr's Float-text test
  lost its C++ expected-failure mark (#34 is fixed), and unit_cxx_emit
  builds the goal of its hand-made module with raw_expr, because that
  module has no FlatCurry interface for the typed builder.

2026-10-05: residuation-style arithmetic over free Int variables (issue #37)
- Diagnosis.  The sendMoreMoney program of the test data suspended on the
  C++ backend and did not end within 120 s on the Python backend; PAKCS
  prints the solution in 2.6 s.  The cause is not in the runtime.  PAKCS
  runs the tool transbooleq over every FlatCurry file before it compiles
  it (currytools/optimize/BindingOpt, called from compiler.pl under the
  pakcsrc property bindingoptimization=fast): a Boolean equality whose
  value is required to be True becomes constrEq.  The guard of main and
  the guards of digit and nzdigit are such conditions.  Under PAKCS the
  digit calls bind the letters and the store slots by constraint, and the
  arithmetic conjuncts suspend on the unbound letters and resume as the
  letters are bound.  With -Dbindingoptimization=no PAKCS reports
  "Evaluation suspended" in 1 s, as the C++ backend did.  Sprite's
  toolchain had no such pass.  The front end writes
  _impl#==#Prelude.Eq#Prelude.Int, which is rigid (eqInt x y =
  (prim_eqInt $# y) $# x), so vm == nzdigit 'M' suspended on vm for good,
  and so did every other conjunct.  With =:= in place of == the C++
  backend solved the program in 0.1 s before any change: the residuation
  support of the runtime (the residuals of a configuration, ready() and
  the rotation, concurrentAnd_step) was not at fault, and PAKCS, the
  oracle, never ran the program as written either.  Facts of the probes:
  under PAKCS, x == 3 and x == 'a' with x free suspend as values and bind
  in a guard; a guard with an otherwise branch, an if-then-else, (||) in a
  guard, (/=) in a guard, and id $ x == 3 suspend; (&&) in a guard, not
  (x /= 3), solve (x == 3), (===), a polymorphic Eq dictionary, and a free
  list bind; (x == 1) & (y == 2) as a value is True and (1 == 2) & True as
  a value has no value, because (&) requires both arguments True whatever
  its result is required to be; not ((x == 3) & (y == 4)) and not (solve
  (x == 3)) as values suspend, and not (not ((x == 3) & (y == 4))) is
  True.  Sprite gives the same seventeen answers on both backends after
  the fix (tests/unit_flat2icurry_bindingopt.py, PARITY_RESULTS).
- Fix.  curry.toolchain.flat2icurry.bindingopt is a port of the fast mode
  of transbooleq: the walk over the rule with a required value (Any, AnyC,
  or a constructor), caseArgType (the second branch False -> failed, or
  the one live branch), the Prelude table of preludeBoolReqValues (&&,
  not, ||, &, solve, &&>) with argumentTypesFor and lubAType, the three
  shapes of an equality call (an instance method with its dictionaries
  first, a class method under apply twice, a default instance method
  under apply twice), the reduction of $ over a partial application, and
  the rebuild of every rule that holds an equality.  The lattice is the
  one of the analysis: the join of two different constructors is the set
  of both (a frozenset), not AnyC, and a set requires nothing of the
  arguments of a call below it.  The first port joined them to AnyC and
  took the join path of the table under it, so not ((x == 3) & (y == 4))
  and not (solve (x == 3)) as values gave False where PAKCS suspends; the
  review of the batch found it.  translate and
  translate_file take bindingopt=False; the build route
  (_frontend.flat2icy) passes True; the oracle tests and the icurry route
  do not, so the oracle stays what icurry wrote.  The command line has
  --bindingopt.  The key of the ICurry cache covers the pass through the
  digest of the port's sources.  A product written before the change is
  reused until its source is newer (the chain keeps the filesys.newer
  rule), so a module of the pool that needs the pass must lose its .icy
  first, or its source must be touched; the review of the batch found the
  pool product of sendMoreMoney in that state in the worktree of the
  batch (it was made before the pass; sprite-exec -m sendMoreMoney
  suspended), and a touch of the source made it again.  The overlay
  archive holds no product of that module.  No test of the suite imports
  it from the pool; the new tests compile their programs from text.
- The Python backend, two defects.  Its primitives on unboxed values
  (_unbox in backends/py/currylib/prelude/math.py) evaluated every
  argument to hnf_or_free before they suspended, so vm == digit 'M' ran
  the generator before it suspended on vm: ten configurations per letter,
  their product for the program.  The C++ backend
  (currylib/defs/unboxed.def) and PAKCS stop at the first free argument.
  Four letters took 101 s on the Python backend and suspend at once now;
  _unbox suspends at the first free argument, from the left.  With that
  alone the puzzle still did not end there, and the first report of the
  batch took it for a speed limit; the review found the cause: ($##)
  (apply_gnf in backends/py/currylib/prelude/apply.py) counted a free
  variable with a binding and no generator as a residual, and _make_ready
  (rts_control.py) releases a configuration whose residual has a binding
  at once, so the step suspended on the same variable again without end.
  show x after x =:= (3 ? 4) spun at full load on the Python backend
  (the C++ backend printed "3" and "4"), and the puzzle finished its
  search (54,557 forks, the count of the C++ backend) and then spun in
  the show of the first digit.  The step now suspends on the variables
  without any information (rts.is_void: no generator, no binding, not
  narrowed), the test of ensureNotFree.  The puzzle then takes about 7 s
