2026-10-06: example 24 restructured, the makefile is the Curry script
- The owner's correction of 2026-10-06: the example was expected to write
  something makefile-like in Curry (CC = ..., object (stem ++ ".o") = ...,
  a small library imported, a makefile drop-in), and Build.curry with the
  project as a Python record did not show it.  examples/24-build-system is
  now three parts.  Make.curry, the library: data Rule = Rule Target
  [Target] [String] (an empty command is a prerequisite line), a makefile
  is a function Target -> Rule, rulesOf mk t = sortValues (set1 mk t)
  collects the rules of a target (the #86 note stays), build reads the
  makefile once from the goal down into the rules of every name of the
  build with the includes beside them (the database of make), and the
  questions read the build: inputs (the merged prerequisites with the
  headers through the include table), stale over the stamp table, waves
  and plan (a cycle fails the guard), ready, affected (anyOf over the
  targets under the guard reads), ambiguous (the rules with a command of
  a target that has more than one), rows for the driver.
  project/Makefile.curry, the makefile in the owner's shape: cc, cflags,
  ar, library, program, the object lists and goal as definitions, the
  pattern rule as the functional pattern rule (stem ++ ".o") = Rule ...,
  the library and the program as guarded equations with t as $@, one
  prerequisite line without a command; project/Makefile_ambiguous.curry
  imports it and adds a second rule with a command for main.o;
  project/Makefile_toolchain.curry binds the compiler as a parameter of
  the rules and lists makefiles = [(cc, rules cc) | cc <- toolchains];
  project/Makefile_choice.curry has cc = "gcc" ? "clang" inside the rules.
  The four C sources and two headers are real files in project/.  make.py
  is generic: -f, -C, -j, -n, --touch, --affected, --trace, --serve, GOAL;
  it imports the makefile from its directory (realpath, so a linked
  makefile compiles beside the original), passes rule as a function value,
  builds one Curry value of the build per process and shares it across
  the questions, scans the includes, reads st_mtime_ns, runs the ready set
  in a thread pool, prints the plan, the waves and the status, with the
  stand-in when a program of the plan is not on the PATH; a module may
  export makefiles, a list of named makefiles, and the driver plans each
  and builds with the first whose programs are on the PATH.  The run
  script copies the sources into a temporary directory, links the
  makefiles, and runs six steps: the full build, nothing to do, the touch
  of shape.h with --affected, the lint, the toolchain plans, the lint on
  the per-use choice.  README, examples/README and Examples.rst follow;
  unit_examples.py keeps 24 on both backends.
