2026-10-05: rotation by a ticker (K1, #68) and the deterministic mode (K2, #69)
- The C++ backend rotates its queue of alternatives in one of two modes,
  selected by the interpreter flag ``rotation`` (flags.py) and by the
  environment variable SPRITE_ROTATION, which sets the flag below
  SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS (whose items now split at their first colon, so
  rotation:time:10ms is one item).  The forms are time:<N><unit> (s, ms,
  us, ns; a decimal is fine) and steps:<N>; both numbers lie between 1 and
  2**63, so that the runtime can hold them (a larger step count failed at
  the first evaluation with a TypeError of pybind11 before the review);
  other text is a ValueError when the interpreter is made
  (flags.parse_rotation, flags.check_flags in Interpreter.__new__), so a
  typo in the variable fails the tool at once with the sentence.  An empty
  SPRITE_ROTATION counts as unset everywhere: the interpreter takes its
  default, and cytest, the runner (cli.environment), the harness
  (Settings.environment) and run-tests.sh set step mode over it (they kept
  an empty value before the review, so SPRITE_ROTATION= ran the tests in
  time mode without a word).  Default: time:10ms for the tools
  (sprite-exec, the REPL, curry.eval, python -m curry).  Step mode,
  steps:65536, is set through the environment by the test runner (so
  SPRITE_ROTATION=time:10ms runs a file in time mode), by cytest before
  the first import of curry (a file run by hand with unittest discover is
  in step mode too), by the benchmark harness (-e SPRITE_ROTATION=time:10ms
  measures time mode), by run-tests.sh (ci.yml's test script) and by the
  env block of perf.yml.  The Python backend keeps its step budget and
  ignores the flag; the docs say so.
- The design as built (cyrt/ticker.hpp, ticker.cpp).  One atomic byte
  g_tick.  A detached thread named cyrt-ticker sets it every quantum with
  a relaxed store; its state (mutex, condition variable, the count of the
  evaluations in time mode on the C stack, the started flag, the quantum,
  the tick count) is a leaked singleton, for the reason the B3 entry
  records (the destructor of a condition variable blocks until its waiters
  left, and a static one hung the exit).  The thread touches g_tick and
  that state alone and ends with the process.  RuntimeState carries the
  cadence (rotation_steps, 0 for time mode; rotation_next;
  rotation_quantum_ns), passed from the flags through the RuntimeStateBase
  constructor like stack_limit; the constructor refuses time mode with a
  quantum of zero (std::invalid_argument, a ValueError in Python), and so
  does ticker_enter, because a thread with no quantum would never release
  its mutex (the Python side always passes at least one nanosecond; the
  guard is for the constructor itself).  check_interrupts: stepcount
  increments in both modes (the concurrent conjunction reads it as its
  progress marker, constr.cpp); in step mode the check is stepcount ==
  rotation_next, then rotation_next += rotation_steps, which reproduces
  the cadence of the mask of today exactly and admits any count; in time
  mode it is a relaxed load of g_tick, cleared when set; the slow path is
  today's: the pending swaps of tiered execution, then the scan of qstack
  for the outermost queue with more than one configuration (E_ROTATE); the
  collection check follows as before.  The outermost procD of a state
  holds a TickerScope in time mode: ticker_enter counts the evaluation and
  starts the thread when the state has none (under the mutex;
  pthread_atfork is registered there once) or wakes it when it is parked;
  a nested procD (a set function) is inside the scope; the count drops at
  exit without a lock.  The thread parks (cv.wait) when it finds the count
  zero at a tick, so an idle host has no wakeups; it sets the tick only
  while the count is positive.  A notify happens only when the thread is
  parked, so a goal with many values costs one uncontended lock per value
  and no context switch of the thread.  When std::thread throws (EAGAIN
  under RLIMIT_NPROC, ENOMEM), ticker_enter takes the count back and
  throws a std::runtime_error that names the ticker and step mode
  ("cannot start the ticker thread of time mode (...); set the rotation to
  step mode, for example SPRITE_ROTATION=steps:65536"), so the next
  evaluation tries again; before the review the count stayed at one, and
  every later evaluation of the process ran without a thread and without
  a rotation after one bare "Resource temporarily unavailable".  Fork:
  the child handler of pthread_atfork gives the child a fresh state (no
  thread, the count of the evaluations in flight carried over, the byte
  clear); the first evaluation entered in the child starts a ticker of
  its own whatever the count, because the state has none (before the
  review only a count of zero started it, so an evaluation entered while
  one carried over from the fork was still in flight, a Python callback
  inside a step that evaluates, ran without a ticker); an evaluation in
  flight at the fork runs in the child without a ticker until then.  A
  host that evaluates in time mode is multi-threaded, so Python warns on a
  later os.fork; the docs say so, and that step mode starts no thread.
  The hook the native workers of family W poll: ticker_due() (and
  ticker_clear()).  The bindings gained ticker_status() (started, parked,
  active, ticks, quantum_ns) and, for the tests, ticker_enter(quantum_ns)
  and ticker_leave(), which do what the outermost scheduler does on entry
  and exit.  The host-interrupt check at the safepoint (open question 7 of
  the plan) is not in: an unwind from the safepoint through generated code
  and the nested schedulers needs its own design.
- Gate K, measured on this machine after the review (12 cores, other
  users' processes, the C++ backend, interpret:off, three repetitions,
  the deterministic columns decide; the tables with the commands are in
  the notebook, perf-program/k-measurement.md):
  the harness, instructions of time mode over step mode: Tak1 16242.9 M
  over 16174.8 M (+0.42 percent), Queens10 18109.8 M over 18019.5 M
  (+0.50), PermSort 18592.9 M over 18557.9 M (+0.19); compare
  --deterministic says "same" for all three with the counters equal (Tak1
  33629915 steps, Queens10 44630642, PermSort 12531076 steps and 1588167
  forks, in both modes), and the CPU seconds of this pair agree within
  three percent (~cpu 0.99, 1.03, 1.00; the first pair, measured during a
  load of the machine, had shown 1.33 to 1.50).  Against the HEAD build
  (the install of the branch at 4e21df99, its own compiled objects of the
  three programs, perf stat -e instructions:u on sprite-exec -t --stats,
  three repetitions each, the modes alternating): HEAD 16071.0 M, step
  mode 16172.0 M (+0.63 percent), time mode 16239.3 M (+1.05) on Tak1;
  17882.9, 18015.6 (+0.74), 18105.5 (+1.24) on Queens10; 18516.7, 18554.5
  (+0.20), 18588.5 (+0.39) on PermSort; the counters equal in all three.
  Per completed step that is 3.0 instructions more in step mode and 5.0
  more in time mode (5.7 on PermSort, whose 500 ticks each scan a long
  qstack).  So time mode is not cheaper than HEAD: the design's "a load
  and a branch replace an increment, a mask, and a branch" did not come
  true, because the increment of stepcount stays for the conjunction and
  the test of the mode was added; the time-mode path is HEAD's increment,
  then a load of rotation_steps, a test and a branch, then a load of the
  address of g_tick through the GOT, a load of the byte, a test and a
  branch, then the collection check (objdump of the staged libcyrt.so).
  The CPU seconds show none of it: the nine perf stat runs per program
  lie within three percent of each other with no order between HEAD and
  the two modes (Tak1 1.23 to 1.31 s, Queens10 1.38 to 1.47 s, PermSort
  4.98 to 5.27 s); the ticker shows as about 100 context switches per
  second (129 to 148 on the 1.3 s programs, 517 to 547 on PermSort).  Two
  ways to take instructions back were left for a later pass: a hidden
  alias of g_tick for the hot path inside libcyrt (the exported byte stays
  for the native workers and the bindings, which include the header), one
  load per step; and the progress marker of the conjunction on
  steps_total, which would free time mode of the increment, three loads
  and stores per step, but changes when a conjunction suspends after a
  step inside hnf.  Latency of fair (loop 0 ? 42) in time mode, 20 probes
  with the thread parked before each: min 10.6, median 10.6, max 16.4 ms;
  in a child of os.fork, 10 probes: 10.6, 10.7, 16.4 ms; all within three
  quanta (step mode takes 4 ms for its 65536 steps).  Idle: after an
  evaluation the thread parks within a quantum, and over two seconds its
  ticks and its context switches do not move (the test reads
  /proc/self/task).  A goal with 40320 values (perm [1..8]) costs the same
  wall and CPU seconds in both modes (0.14 to 0.15 s, the first
  measurement); the extra voluntary context switches are the ticks.
