2026-10-06: the binding optimization rewrites the FlatCurry file (#85)
- Cause.  Since 1b554fd1 the build route applied the pass in memory
  (translate_file(..., bindingopt=True)) while the icurry route and the
  oracle translated the file as the front end wrote it: the two routes
  wrote different .icy files for a module with a required Boolean
  equality, and the on-disk oracle check accepted a product of the build
  route only through a second translation (accept_build_route, 4e21df99).
  PAKCS applies the optimization to the FlatCurry file itself:
  preprocessFcyFile in compiler.pl runs BindingOpt, which writes M.fcy_OPT
  and renames it over M.fcy when an equality was replaced.
- Fix.  A FlatCurry writer in the format of the front end, the show of
  Haskell (terms.showhaskell; flatcurry.show and flatcurry.write).  The
  PAKCS writer showterm does not serve: the front end writes Prog "M" ...
  without the outer parentheses, "" for the empty string, the ASCII names
  \NUL to \DEL and \SO\&H in a string, and 1.0e7 for a float.  The writer
  round-trips every .fcy of the corpus byte for byte, the 1260 files
  under tests/data and the 1210 files of the archive, the library
  included, on its first run; func_flat2icurry.py pins it
  (test_writer_round_trip), and unit_flat2icurry.py tests the escapes,
  the numbers and the parentheses (TestHaskellWriter) and the sample, the
  probes and the library modules of the archive (TestFlatCurryWriter).
  The rewrite step, bindingopt.optimize_file: both routes run it after
  the front end and before the translation (_frontend.curry2icurry; the
  icurry branch of Curry2ICurryConverter.convert runs the front end and
  the rewrite before the icurry program, whose own front-end run finds
  its files current and leaves the rewritten file alone, checked by the
  time of the file).  The file is written again when the pass replaced
  an equality, as transbooleq writes it; a rule whose equalities all stay
  is rebuilt (its $ reduced) only then, so optimize_bindings returns the
  program itself when the count is zero (the Prelude is such a program:
  no replacement, a rebuilt rule).  The translation runs with
  bindingopt=False on both routes, with icurry_compat as before.  The
  pass is idempotent over the archive: a second run replaces nothing and
  gives the same program, and the optimized program survives the writer
  and the reader (test_rewrite_is_idempotent).  The cache digest of the
  icurry route covers the sources of the pass (cache.REWRITE_SOURCES).
- Decisions.  --bindingopt of python -m curry.toolchain.flat2icurry stays
  the in-memory pass: it translates a file the routes have not rewritten
  as the routes would, and leaves the file alone.  Two hand runs:
  python -m curry.toolchain.flat2icurry.rewrite M.fcy... rewrites
  FlatCurry files in place (a module of its own, so python -m gives no
  warning about a module the package imported), and sprite-make
  --rewrite-flat M runs the step from Curry to ICurry again, current or
  not, without the ICurry cache (a hit writes no FlatCurry), and makes
  the ICurry and the later products from the rewritten file; it runs in
  this process (--jobs 1) and needs the source.
- The oracle.  test_products_on_disk runs without the fallback;
  accept_build_route is gone.  Before the change, with the fallback off,
  two of the 1258 pairs on disk in this worktree differed, CxxInterp and
  CxxScheduler: an .icy of the in-memory pass beside the front end's
  .fcy.  No product differed through icurry_compat alone, so no fallback
  for that setting is kept.  The hand run rewrote the two files (one
  equality each), and the check passes.  The pass changes 7 of the 1260
  files under tests/data (CxxInterp, CxxScheduler, atableNoflex,
  sendMoreMoney with 15 equalities, BFSvsPar, RegExp,
  poker_four_of_a_kind) and some files of the archive.
- What stays as it is.  The committed ICurry of the Curry library
  predates the pass and is not regenerated: make stage derives the JSON
  from the committed .icy and never translates a library module unless
  SPRITE_REBUILD_ICY=1; that run would now rewrite the library's .fcy
  under curry/lib/.curry and translate the optimized program.  The
  archive's .fcy files are the front end's and pair with the .icy files
  icurry wrote from them; a rebuilt archive packs rewritten .fcy files
  with icurry's translation of them.  A product written before this
  change keeps its .fcy as the front end wrote it until the source is
  newer (the front end then writes the file again and the route rewrites
  it) or a hand run rewrites it; an .icy of the pre-pass era is remade
  only by sprite-make --rewrite-flat or a newer source.  In this worktree
  the .fcy and the .icy of sendMoreMoney under tests/data are both
  unoptimized (made before the pass) and stay so; the test compiles the
  program from text.  A .fcy that PAKCS rewrote in place (the oracle
  runs PAKCS on the test modules, and its BindingOpt writes the showTerm
  format) is not the front end's text: the reader reads it, the pass
  finds nothing to replace, and the round-trip test leaves such a file
  out; the corpus holds none today.
- Verification, one file per process under prlimit --as 6 GB, both
  backends: unit_flat2icurry.py 77 OK (2 skipped),
  unit_flat2icurry_bindingopt.py 31 OK, unit_curry2icurry.py 15 OK (the
