2026-10-06: a functional pattern binds a free argument (=:<= against a free variable)
- Symptom.  A function with a functional pattern, called with a free
  variable in that argument position, gave no value on the C++ backend and
  EvaluationSuspended on the Python backend, where PAKCS binds the
  variable: with rule (stem ++ ".o") [stem ++ ".c"] = "cc", the goal
  rule "vec.o" ins where ins free is {ins=["vec.c"]} "cc" in PAKCS 3.4.1.
  The desugaring written out, rule7 (stem ++ ".o") ins | [stem ++ ".c"]
  =:<= ins, failed the same way; the variable on the pattern side (rule8,
  ins =:<= [stem ++ ".c"]) and the strict guard (rule1, ins =:= [...])
  bound it, and the ground call rule "vec.o" ["vec.c"] answered.  The
  owner met it on the pattern rule of example 24, through a set function
  over a free variable created inside the function: an empty set.
- Cause.  The front end matches the outer constructors of the argument
  by cases, which narrow a free argument, and writes the functional
  sub-patterns as one =:<= with the pattern on the left: rule_FREE1 is
  cond ((stem ++ ".o", stem ++ ".c") =:<= (x, y)) "cc" with stem free.
  So the step of =:<= meets a constructor or a primitive value on the
  left and a free variable on the right.  nonstrictEq_step (constr.cpp)
  narrowed the variable over the type of the left side without a guide,
  rts->hnf(C, &rhs, lhs.target->info->type).  The type of Char, Int or
  Float has one constructor, the value node, and instantiate made a
  generator from it: Node::create_flat writes a fresh variable into the
  one slot, which is unboxed, so the pattern 'v' met a Char node with a
  pointer for its value, _equate_rec compared the two and failed, and no
  alternative was left.  nonstrict_eq (constr.py) called
  rhs.hnf(typedef=lhs.typedef) with no values, and hnf suspends a free
  variable of a builtin type without values.  =:= had neither defect:
  constrEq_step passes make_guides (the one value of a primitive, or the
  type of a constructor) and constr_eq passes the value.
- Fix.  nonstrictEq_step passes make_guides for a free right side, as
  constrEq_step does: a free Char, Int or Float takes a value binding
  (ValueBinding, as under =:=), and a free variable of a data type is
  narrowed over the constructors of the type as before.  The codes with
  an unboxed side are covered too: a free side against an unboxed value
  takes the value (make_guides reads an unboxed cursor; its assert on
  'p' is gone), and the one error left, an unboxed value against a node,
  cannot occur, because the two sides of one =:<= have one
  representation (the format of =:<= is "pp", and _equate_rec compares
  the primitive types itself before it recurses); no test reaches it.  A
  free left side (the NonStrictConstraint path) and two free variables
  are unchanged.  nonstrict_eq passes the value of a builtin type to hnf,
  as constr_eq does.  apply_binding, the other maker of =:<= nodes, puts
  a generator on the left, whose head normal form is a constructor, so
  its path is the same as before.
- Tests.  tests/data/curry/FunPatFreeArg.curry: rule, rule7, rule8,
  rule1, cand f t = (ins, f t ins) where ins free, count (xs ++ [0])
  [length xs] over Int lists, wrap n (Just (n + 1)), double (Circle r)
  (Rect (2 * r) (2 * r)) over a small data type, upper (c : _) (toUpper
