2026-10-06: G8, the growth factor of the collector (#83)
- The knob.  The collector of the default build (make GC=wdgc;
  graph/memory.cpp includes gc/blockheap.cpp and gc/wdgc.cpp unless
  SPRITE_GC_MPS is defined) sets its node threshold after a collection to
  the growth factor times the survivors, not below the configured
  threshold (2^20 nodes), and its configuration threshold to the factor
  times the live configurations, not below one eighth of the configured
  threshold.  So the heap stays within the factor times the live nodes,
  and the mark cost per allocation is 1 over the factor minus 1.
  GC_DEFAULT_GROWTH is 8; SPRITE_GC_GROWTH overrides it when the runtime
  loads.  gc/mps.cpp (make GC=mps, the experiment behind a gate) replaces
  both files in a build and never links into the default one; its
  g_growth (8, floor 2) reads the same variable and scales the
  configuration threshold after a full collection alone, since MPS
  schedules its own collections.  So the two defaults are one knob for
  the configuration threshold only.
- Measured: the memory suite of the harness (run_benchmarks -s memory
  --variant collector=on -b cxx -r 3 -w 1 --cap 4G --timeout 600 -e
  SPRITE_GC_GROWTH=N -e SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=interpret:off, the 28
  dissertation programs that compile on this machine, PokerChoice and
  PokerFree need currypp, plus QueensSet9) at growth 8, 12 and 16: three
  measured repetitions after a warm-up and one under perf for the
  instructions, the modules compiled by sprite-make --so first, one
  harness invocation per factor and one program at a time, on a machine
  shared with two other lanes (load average 1.9 to 4.1).  Collections,
  nodes marked and steps are exact and agree between the repetitions; the
  instructions are the one perf repetition of each item, deterministic
  within the tolerance of the compare tool (1 percent); the seconds are
  indicative.  The factor touches a program only when the survivors of a
  collection times the factor exceed the threshold floor (131,072
  survivors at growth 8, 65,536 at 16), or when the live configurations
  times the factor exceed one eighth of the floor, since the
  configuration threshold follows the same rule: 18 of the 29 programs
  kept their collections and their nodes marked exactly at every factor,
  with the same peak RSS, among them Queens10 (38 collections), Primes
  (47), Tak1 (30), Psort (283) and Quicksort (96); Horseman and QueensSet
  kept their collections, and QueensSet, below the node floor, moved its
  nodes marked by 4 percent and its peak RSS by 8 percent through the
  configuration threshold.  On the nine others the trade is proportional,
  as the policy makes it.  Growth 16 against 8:
  collections QueensSet9 11 to 6, SearchQueens 19 to 10, PermSort 9 to
  5, PermSortPeano 10 to 5, SearchMAC 78 to 60, Hamming 4 to 3, Fib,
  ReverseBuiltin and ReverseHO 2 to 1; nodes marked -50 percent on the
  four search programs, -29 on SearchMAC, -85 on the two Reverse
  programs; the collector's seconds QueensSet9 1.363 to 1.044 (-23
  percent; the destruction of its dead queues is a per-run term, G9),
  SearchQueens 0.890 to 0.494 (-44), PermSort 0.169 to 0.083 (-51),
  PermSortPeano 0.194 to 0.103 (-47), SearchMAC 0.609 to 0.447 (-27),
  which is 1 to 8 percent of the CPU of those programs; the
  instructions -3 percent on QueensSet9 and SearchQueens, -2 on
  PermSort, -1 on PermSortPeano, SearchMAC and Hamming, -6 on Fib, -10 on
  ReverseBuiltin and ReverseHO, 22 programs the same within 1 percent
  (compare --deterministic); the CPU seconds within the noise (QueensSet9
  0.95, SearchQueens 1.00, PermSort 1.03).  Peak RSS +43 to +53 percent
  on the four programs with a live set near 300 MiB (QueensSet9 330 to
  473 MiB, SearchQueens 307 to 465, PermSort 314 to 481, PermSortPeano
  307 to 468), SearchMAC 66 to 88, Hamming 159 to 168; over the suite the
  sum of the peaks 3557 to 4254 MiB (+20 percent) for 52 percent fewer
  nodes marked (40.6 M to 19.3 M) and 33 percent fewer collector seconds
  (3.79 to 2.55 s).  Growth 12 sits halfway on both sides: -30 percent
  nodes marked, -21 percent collector seconds, +8 percent RSS in sum
  (+22 to +29 on the four large programs).
- Repeated at the end for the two factors that decide, 8 and 16 (the
  records -rerun2 and, for QueensSet9 alone, -rerun3 in g8-records/).
  The other lane of the worktree had put its optimizer pass into the
  shared install meanwhile, so the steps of 28 programs changed between
  the first run and the repeat (Queens10 44,630,642 to 34,164,285,
  SearchQueens 39,256,210 to 35,529,964), and the repeat is a second
  observation of the trade on that state, both factors on the same
  steps; a first repeat failed on a module of that lane that the install
  did not link yet, and one repetition of QueensSet9 at growth 16 died
  with SIGBUS when a product was rewritten under it, so QueensSet9 ran
  alone once more.  The exact columns agree with the first run
  (collections PermSort 9 to 5, PermSortPeano 10 to 5, SearchMAC 78 to
  60, QueensSet9 11 to 6, SearchQueens 19 to 9 on its new steps; nodes
  marked -50 to -53 percent on the four search programs; the
  instruction ratios within half a percent of the first run), the
  collector seconds of growth 16 against 8 are 0.68 on QueensSet9 (1.555
  to 1.059 s), 0.51 on SearchQueens, 0.54 on PermSort, 0.62 on
  PermSortPeano and 0.84 on SearchMAC, and the peak RSS is +43 to +52
  percent on the four large programs (QueensSet9 333 to 477 MiB).  The
  CPU seconds do not follow the instructions (PermSort 1.17 and
  PermSortPeano 1.22 on 2 and 1 percent fewer instructions, QueensSet9
  alone 0.85 on 2.7 percent fewer; the load average 3.5 to 7.0): they
  are indicative, the exact columns decide.
- Decision: the default stays at 8.  The work saved and the memory added
  are the same proportion on every program the factor touches, so no
  factor is a free gain; growth 16 buys half the marking work with half
  again the peak RSS of the programs that use the most memory, and it
  doubles the bound the design states (the heap within the growth factor
  times the live nodes); the saving is 1 to 3 percent of the instructions
  of a run, and the 18 programs below the floor gain nothing at any
  factor; the counters of every nightly record stay where they are.  A
  user with memory to spare buys the saving with SPRITE_GC_GROWTH=16.
  Docs: the SPRITE_GC_GROWTH entry of the environment variables page and
  the collector section of the benchmark README state the measured
  trade, and name the configuration threshold as the second path by
  which the factor touches a program (the review of 2026-10-06 found the
  first text stated the node floor alone).  No code and no test changed;
  unit_cxx_gc.py pins the default (test_growth,
  test_threshold_follows_the_survivors): 23 tests OK on the C++ backend.
  The table, the records, the commands and the load are in the
  perf-program note g8-growth.md and g8-records/ of the notebook.
- Left: the per-run term of QueensSet9 (G9); a benchmark with an old,
  retained live set, where the factor would trade differently (design
  open question 2); the Poker programs need currypp on the machine to
  join the suite.

2026-10-06: O2 and O3, the inliner and the known constructor (#71, #72)
- The pass inline_calls (interpreter/optimize.py; the analysis in
  icurry/analysis/inlining.py) runs between saturate_applies and
  inline_aliases under the key <backend>.opt.inline_calls.  Two rules
  rewrite a saturated call in place, from the inside out, and the result
  is rewritten again (to the depth MAX_DEPTH, 16; the dictionary chain
  takes about five): a call of a non-recursive function whose body is an
  expression of at most inline_budget nodes (a new interpreter flag,
  default 4, 0 turns the pass off; the budget counts the nodes a body
  builds, calls, partial applications, constructors, choices and
  literals, a variable counts nothing) is replaced by the body; a call
  g (K a b) of a non-recursive function whose body is one case on that
  parameter, with a branch for K, is replaced by the body of the branch
  (a branch of at most CASE_LIMIT, 32, nodes), with the fields for the
  pattern variables.  An apply whose head the rewrites expose is
  saturated with analysis.saturate.  The sharing rule (plan 4.3): an
  argument, a field, the whole constructor (when the branch uses the
  parameter as a whole), or a local of the body that is used more than
  once is bound first to a fresh variable of the caller's block (IVarDecl
  and IVarAssign, inserted before the statement), so that it is built
  once; one used once is written at its use; one not used is dropped; a
  variable, a literal and a static string are written at every use.  A
  free variable of the body is a fresh IFreeDecl of the caller's block
  (one per call site, as one per call before; unknown inlines to a fresh
  free variable).  A choice in the body is one choice per call site, as
  one call per site.  A body may bind locals (a let that is not
  recursive); a body with a recursive let (INodeAssign) is neither
  inlined nor rewritten (the patch names a path of a built node).  A body
  with a case, other than the one case of the second rule, a branch with
  a nested case or an exempt, a path of two entries, and a variable the
  body does not bind are not shapes (analysis.body_shape); the case at a
  return position is O4's.  An alias function keeps its body, so
  inline_aliases records its target as before; its callers get the alias
  inlined (one node), then the target.  A call of a user alias of apply
  (app f x = f x) stays, as it stays for saturate_applies: the source
  wrote the indirection, and unit_cxx_partial.py needs it to hand a known
  function value to the apply of the runtime (the first form inlined app
  and saturated the chain, and four tests there lost their subject).
- The body that replaces a call is the body as it was before the pass
  rewrote the callee (analysis.inline_shape copies it when first asked,
  which the pass does before it rewrites a function), after O1: the inner
  calls are then rewritten where the arguments are known.  So x /= y on
  Int resolves: _def#/= inlines to not (apply (apply (== d) x) y), ==
  to ==._#lambda (apply d ()), the apply saturates to _inst#Eq#Int (),
  the known-constructor rule gives the dictionary, ==._#lambda selects
  _impl#==, the applies saturate, and _impl#== inlines to eqInt x y;
  mod x n /= 0 resolves likewise through _def#mod, its local pair,
  divMod, _impl#divMod (the pair (divInt, modInt)) and the selector of
  the second component, to not (eqInt (modInt x n) 0).  Non-recursive
  means not on a cycle of saturated calls within the module (Tarjan over
  IFCall edges; a cycle cannot cross modules); a partial application is
  a value and makes no edge, since the dictionary of an instance names
  its methods and a method that falls back to a class default names the
  dictionary, and the chain needs both inlined.
- Each function records the body a call of it is replaced by under
  all.inline_body as ICurry-JSON (the whole IFuncBody), when the function
  is public, non-recursive and within the limits (RECORD_LIMIT 8 for an
  expression body, CASE_LIMIT 32 for a branch), or when it is private and
  a recorded body of the module names it, in a call or in a partial
  application (analysis.recorded_functions): the selector behind a class
  method and the lambda behind . are private, and the chain needs them.
  So a module loaded from its compiled form, which has no bodies, still
  tells its small functions to its importers; both backends carry the
  metadata in their records (the C++ loader hands the keys and the
  metadata on since O1).  The Prelude records 1246 of its 1670 functions
  (664 expression bodies, 582 single-case bodies, 1.04 MB of text in
  Prelude.so and Prelude.py); the record is what the warm expression
  compile pays for (+4.5 ms).  A copied body that names a symbol of a
  module the caller does not import makes the module join the imports.
- Staleness: the format stamps went to 12 (C++) and 6 (Python), so make
  stage and the first import write every cached module again, once; a
  file of format 11 or 5 is correct but slower, and its record tells the
  modules that import it no bodies.  Found on the way: the product
  directory keeps a product whose stamp is current whatever version of a
  pass wrote it, so the background compiles of the tiered mode during the
  work on the pass left objects of intermediate forms in the install and
  in the benchmark directory, which the first two measurements of the
  table measured; the products were deleted and compiled again.
- Found on the way, two: the C++ emitter could not assign a free variable
  to a Variable (_1 = _2 with _2 a Node * from rts->freshvar()), a shape
  the front end never wrote and the inlining of unknown into a let
  writes ($1 <- freeVal with freeVal = x where x free).  passthrough.py
  no longer lets a free variable on the right side force a Variable on
  the left side (a plain left side copies the pointer), and the emitter
  gives a Variable on the left side the node through its target, as for
  a node the step builds (passthrough.free_variables; compiler.free_vars).
  The bytecode and the Python emitters handled the shape.
- Measured with the harness on the C++ backend, 3 repetitions, perf, other
