2026-10-07: a set function over a variable a functional pattern bound (#97),
  and the measurement of #86 after it
- Symptom (the issue, filed at 15:14 PDT on 2026-10-06 from the exploration
  of example 24).  Module Probe2: g (s ++ ".c") = length s, inner x =
  sortValues (set1 g x), direct (s ++ ".o") = length (inner (s ++ ".c")),
  main4 = [direct "vec.o"]: std::bad_alloc after six seconds at the 2 GB
  cap on the C++ backend, where the Python backend answers [1].  On this
  branch (d54c092b) the module answers on both backends: main3 and main4
  give [1] in 0.3 s (297 and 285 steps, 29 forks), in step mode and in
  time mode, under interpret:new and interpret:all, and with inline_budget
  0.  The fix of #98 (321111e7, merged an hour after the issue was filed)
  changed the path a Char bound outside the capsule takes inside it, and
  hid the symptom for this module.  The two defects behind it stayed, and
  other shapes reach them on this branch.
- Cause one: the capsule does not see the bindings of the enclosing
  configuration.  A binding is private to the configuration that made it
  (Configuration::bindings).  The capsule of a set function is a nested
  configuration with an empty map, and get_binding read that map alone,
  where the decisions of the enclosing configurations are read through the
  queue stack (read_fp, walk_qstack), and where the comments of evalS_step
  and holds_private_state describe the values of the nested evaluation as
  depending on the bindings of the configuration that runs it.  So a
  variable of the
  argument that the enclosing configuration bound looked unbound inside
  the capsule.  Against a literal, a Char or an Int was bound anew, to the
  literal, and the comparison held whatever the outer value: with endsInC
  (t ++ "c") = length t and outerChar (s ++ ".o") = sortValues (set1
  endsInC s), outerChar "cx.o" gave [1] on both backends (PAKCS 3.4.1 on
  the plain goal: no value).  Against a constructor, a list was narrowed:
  instantiate put a generator on the shared variable node, the choice
  escaped (its id joined the escape set through the guard), and the
  enclosing configuration, which never decided it, forked on it.
- Cause two: fork applies a binding without end.  A choice with the id of a
  variable is the generator of that variable, and fork applies the binding
  of the variable to each alternative, (generator =:<= binding) &> e
  (apply_binding), so that the side agrees with the binding.  The
  constraint pull-tabs the same generator node to the root, the
  configuration forks on it again, update_fp finds its side already in the
  fingerprint and says yes, apply_binding wraps the root again, and so on:
  one configuration clone, two pull-tabs and the wrap per round, no step
  ever completes the alternative.  The docstring of the Python step says
  "Pop a binding ... That binding will be removed from the table"; neither
  implementation removed it.  The Python step named the symbol
  rts.prelude.nonstrictEq, which the Prelude handle does not expose (the
  function is private), so it raised AttributeError whenever it ran: the
  Python backend never applied a binding at a fork.  Before #98 the
  unguided narrowing of a bound Char inside the capsule made such a
  generator (the garbage Char of the #98 entry), which is the route the
  module of the issue took to the loop (read from the code of e52ece9d,
  not run).
- Measured on this branch before the fix, sprite-exec --stats, step mode,
  prlimit 2 GB, timeout 120.  endsIn0 (ys ++ [0]) = length ys and
  outerList (id xs) = sortValues (set1 endsIn0 xs): outerList [1, 2, 0]
  std::bad_alloc after 4.8 s at 1.86 GB, 20734141 steps, 6911375 forks, 3
  collections; outerTail (id (x : xs)) = sortValues (set1 endsIn0 xs) on
  the same list: 7.9 s, 1.95 GB, 30086332 steps, 10028771 forks; outerInt
  (id (x : xs)) = sortValues (set1 endsIn5 (x : xs)) with endsIn5 (ys ++
  [5]) = length ys, on [3, 5] and on [5, 3]: the same counts as outerTail.
  Without a set function, sibling = (x =:<= [1, 2] &> (length x ? 7)) ?
  len0 x where x free (len0 by two rules): the C++ backend prints 7, 1, 0
  and dies on the alternative that reads x (20734140 steps, 6911380
  forks); the Python backend raises on every one of these goals ("module
  'Prelude' has no attribute 'nonstrictEq'").  outerChar "cx.o": [1] on
  both backends.
- The fix, on both backends.  (1) get_binding reads through the queue
  stack as read_fp does: the map of the configuration, then the map of the
  front configuration of each enclosing queue, under the group id of the
  variable in that configuration (RuntimeState::get_binding in
  rts_freevars.cpp; get_binding and has_binding of rts_bindings.py through
  _find_binding).  is_void and holds_private_state read through it, so a
  nested configuration that waits on a variable the outside bound is
  ready, and a capsule whose goal holds such a variable is private.  The
  writers (add_binding, apply_binding, update_binding) use the map of the
  configuration alone.  (2) apply_binding consumes the binding it applies
  (write(C->bindings).erase; del config.bindings.write[gid]): the
  constraint carries it into the variables of the generator, and a
  configuration that forks on the generator again applies nothing.  The
  Python step names the symbol through rts.symbol('Prelude.nonstrictEq').
  After the fix every goal above gives the value of the oracle on both
  backends in about 0.2 s: outerList [2] (84 steps, 7 forks), outerTail
  [1], outerInt [1] and [], sibling 0, 1, 2, 7, outerChar "cx.o" [] and
  "xc.o" [1]; the module of the issue [3], [4], [1], [1] (main3 270 steps
  and 24 forks on the C++ backend, 297 and 29 before: the capsule reads
  the bindings of the stem instead of binding its characters anew).
- Tests: tests/unit_setfunctions_bindings.py over
  tests/data/curry/SetFunctionsBindings.curry, five tests on both
  backends, the children under prlimit 1 GB and timeout 120 (a regression
  spins inside C++): the four goals of the issue, the bound Char with its
  plain goals, the bound Int field and tail, the bound list in both forms,
  and the sibling alternatives.  The expected values are those of PAKCS
  3.4.1 on the plain goals (plainChar "cx.o" no value, "xc.o" 1; the list
  goals 2 and 1; sibling 2, 7, 0, 1 through len0, since a case on the free
  variable suspends in PAKCS, where a rule narrows).  Two tests more after
  the review: test_private_capsule (the bound character read by a case
  and by a comparison inside a private capsule, with the plain goals), and
  test_shared_capsule, a known failure (unittest.expectedFailure) on both
  backends, which asserts the values of the plain goals for the three
  shared shapes of the item left below.
- #86 after #97.  The shape of unit_funcpat_narrowed.py measured again with
  curry.stats on both backends (one, many and distinct through source,
  sourceS and sourceN): every count equal before and after the fix.  C++:
  many 219524 steps and 1202 forks through source, 12102 and 8 through
  sourceS, 177745 and 1202 through sourceN; distinct 9529/324, 1690/72,
  10231/450; one 145, 138, 162 steps, 8 forks each.  Python: many 12867,
  12882, 13468 steps with 1202, 8, 1202 forks; distinct 1333/324, 1504/72,
  1594/450.  The cause of #97 was not a resolution the capsule performs
  once; it was the read of the enclosing bindings and the loop at the
  fork, both outside the restart per read of #86 (one configuration, its
  own bindings, copy_spine from the root at every read).  So the check the
  plan asked for does not apply, and nothing changed for #86: the idiom
  and the pinned counters stay; the options of the entry of 2026-10-06
  stand.
- Left, the owner's item.  A capsule shared by enclosing configurations
  that bind a variable of its argument differently after the capsule
  started: the first configuration that runs the capsule puts its binding
  into the private spine of the nested configuration (copy_spine and
  E_RESTART, as for its own binding), the value goes into the shared
  allValues node, and the other configuration reads a value made with the
  first's binding.  Measured with the shapes of section 5 of the test
  module, on both backends, on this build and on the build of d54c092b:
  gCase c = 0 ? hCase c with hCase a case on c, gEq with a comparison
  c == 'a', the capsule started by select while c is free, then (c =:<=
  'a' ? c =:<= 'b') &> selectValue rest.  Before this change both shared
  goals suspended ("Evaluation Suspended!"); after it both alternatives
  give (0, 1), where the plain goals give (0, 1) and (0, 2) (PAKCS 3.4.1
  agrees).  So for these shapes the change replaces a suspension by a
  wrong value in the sharing configuration.  The functional-pattern shape
  (gPat s = 0 ? endsInA s, s bound to "xa" or "xb" after the start) died
  before the change, in the loop of cause two on the C++ backend and in
  the AttributeError on the Python backend, and gives (0, 1) twice after
  it, where the plain goal gives (0, 1) alone.  The private shapes (c
  bound before the capsule starts) suspended before the change and give
  the values of the plain goals after it.  The escape of #61 does not
  cover the shared capsule: a binding has no choice node to escape.  Two
  routes.  (a) The
  pre-#98 route made sound: the capsule does not read an enclosing
  binding of a data type; it narrows the variable, the generator choice
  escapes, and the enclosing fork applies the binding once (the fixed
  apply_binding); sound for a shared capsule, one split and one restart
  per bound variable the capsule meets, and no route for a Char, an Int
  or a Float, which have no generator (a ValueBinding is a constraint, not
  a choice; a choice over it needs the machinery of get_generator and
  constrain_equal).  (b) An ownership test for the capsule (private or
  shared), which the runtime lacks (the entry of #61): a private capsule
  reads the bindings as now, a shared one takes route (a).  The walk of
  this entry is the rule of the dissertation's comments made true for the
  private capsule, which holds_private_state makes of every capsule whose
  argument holds a bound variable at creation; a clone made after the
  creation shares the spine, so it holds the same capsule.
