- #96, the warning on overlapping rules.  The front end prints its
  warnings on stderr in blocks (file:line:col-line:col Warning:, the
  text indented, a blank line between blocks); -W none -W overlapping
  keeps the overlap warning alone (--no-overlap-warn did not silence it
  in the probe, --no-warn does); _system.pexec dropped the stderr of a
  run that succeeded, so no warning reached anyone whatever the flags.
  PAKCS shows the warning by default (parseProgram of c2p.pl;
  warnoverlapping=yes in pakcsrc.default).  The fix: pexec(with_stderr)
  returns the pair; command() adds WARNING_FLAGS (--no-verb -W none -W
  overlapping) unless the caller asked for quiet or
  SPRITE_FRONTEND_WARNINGS says off (0, off, no, false; an empty value
  counts as unset), else QUIET_FLAGS as before; curry2flat groups the
  blocks by source file (warnings_by_file) and logs one record per module
  at the WARNING level through the logger of _frontend ("the Curry front
  end warns on module M (path):" and the text of the front end), which
  the basicConfig of curry.config shows on stderr by default with its
  time stamp.  A module compiled from its products or from the ICurry
  cache runs no front end and warns no more; a run that compiles an
  import again warns on it again.  The other warnings (non-exhaustive
  patterns, missing signatures, unused bindings) stay quiet.  The suites:
  cytest, the runner (cli.environment) and the benchmark harness
  (Settings.environment) set SPRITE_FRONTEND_WARNINGS=0 unless the
  environment sets it; the prepare pass runs sprite-make -q anyway; the
  CI script needs no change, cytest covers every file.  Example 24:
  make.py sets the variable to 0 before it imports curry, because the
  rules of a makefile overlap by design, so ./run shows no warning and
  expected.out does not change for #96; the other examples show the
  warning on stderr on a cold run, which unit_examples.py does not
  compare.  Tests: unit_frontend_warnings.py (the parser and the
  grouping; curry.import_ warns once with the module name and not on the
  non-exhaustive function, then from the products and from the cache not
  at all; curry.compile once and a quiet process not at all; sprite-make
  once on stderr, a second run, -q and the variable not at all; the
  default of cytest in a child process); test_command of
  unit_curry2icurry.py pins the flags.  A first version of the test
  evaluated build 3 with build 0 = []; build n = n : build (n - 1), the
  trap of #36: the second alternative of build 0 is infinite, the
  conversion to Python never ended, and the file ran for nine minutes
