2026-10-07: the ABI stamp names the installation as text, and the product cache (worktree wt/cache, issue #100)
- The stamp.  object_digest no longer digests the real path of the
  installation prefix.  The digest covers what decides whether an object
  fits a runtime: the runtime headers (runtime_digest), the flags of the
  flavor and of the collector with its write counters, the link flags,
  the compiler of the build and the format of the generated code (the
  last two since the review of the item, below).  The stamp file
  <module>.so.abi has two lines now: the digest, 16 hex digits, and the
  real path of the installation (installation_path).  Cpp2So.is_stale
  accepts an object when the digest is one of accepted_digests and the
  second line, as a real path, is the installation of the process
  (stamp_is_current); a stamp of one line, the old form, is stale.  So a
  copy of an installation made by hand stays stale (its stamps name the
  original, whose shared objects its objects link against), a package
  whose manager rewrote the text keeps its objects (conda-build lists a
  text file that holds the prefix in info/has_prefix by itself; the next
  build of the conda package must show 12 binary and 13 text entries,
  and the build prefix must be a real path for conda-build to find it in
  the stamps), and two installations of one runtime give one digest,
  which the product cache keys on.  The format is documented on the
  class Cpp2So.  Consequence: every compiled product is compiled once
  more after this lands (the prepare pass and make stage do it); the 43
  modules of the pool of this worktree took 26.5 s with ccache disabled.
  Tests (unit_cxx_toolchain.py, 36 OK on cxx): TestAbiStamp.
  test_stamp_lines (the reader; a stamp of one line, blank space, a third
  line), test_relocated_object_is_kept (the text rewritten, also through
  a link: no compile), test_hand_copy_is_stale (the right digest with
  another path: compiled again, with a stamp of this installation);
  TestFlavor.test_digest asserts now that the digest does not change
  with the prefix and that the text decides.  conda/README.md closes
  open question 2 for the stamp (the split of the package stays open),
  meta.yaml says that the stamps are text entries, build.sh turns the
  product cache off for the build.  The docs: CompilationPipeline,
  WithoutCompiler, sprite-make, the environment variables.
- The cache.  curry.toolchain._productcache; the root is
  SPRITE_PRODUCT_CACHE (config.product_cache_dir): by default
  $XDG_CACHE_HOME/sprite/products or ~/.cache/sprite/products; the empty
  string turns it off, and off is the behaviour before the cache.  An
  entry is <root>/<digest>/<key>/ with <Module>.cpp, <Module>.so and
  <Module>.so.abi.  The digest is the first line of the stamp.  The key
  (_productcache.product_key, KEY_FORMAT 2 since the review below) digests, in order: the
  format of the key; the name of the module (the generated code and the
  symbols of the object carry it; the math corpus holds pairs of modules
  with one text, intmath065 and intmath073 among them, which shared an
  entry and missed in turn until the name joined the key); the facts of
  the backend (Cpp2So.product_facts):
  the format version of the generated code (compiler.FORMAT_VERSION), a
  digest of the sources of the code generator (the C++ emitter and its
  helpers, the generic emitter, the optimizer and the ICurry analyses:
  GENERATOR_SOURCES), the keys of the optimizer passes, the inline budget
  of the interpreter, the intermediate directory, the digest of the route
  from Curry to ICurry (cache.frontend_digest), the real path of the
  installation (the second line of the stamp) and the real path of the
  directory of the source; and the chain digest: the module's own texts
  (the Curry source, the ICurry file and the JSON, each one that exists;
  since the review below) and the name and the text of every module it imports,
  transitively, found in the Curry path or in the library of the
  installation (the Prelude is implicit).  These are the facts that
  decide staleness today (the stamp, the format stamp of the .cpp, the
  texts of the chain) plus the facts that shape the generated code.  The
  two paths are in the key because an object names them: its record
  names the source (relative to the installation for a library module,
  absolute otherwise), and its NEEDED entries name the objects of its
  imports by absolute path (readelf -d of Numeric.so of the install:
  .../install/curry/.curry/sprite-pakcs-3.4.1/Prelude.so and two more).
  An object of another tree restored here would make the dynamic linker
  map that tree's Prelude beside this one (finding C6 of the audit of
  #82).  So the cache serves one tree: the same tree after its products
  were removed, after an edit and its revert, after a branch switch and
  back.  A second worktree gets no entry of the first, and the
  acceptance of #100 (the prepare pass of a second worktree in under a
  minute) is out of reach under the risk line of the item.  What would
  let two trees share, the owner's decision (the link and the record are
  the runtime's design): the link line names the imports by SONAME (the
  full module name) instead of by path, with the imports loaded before
  the module as the loader does today, and the record of a source
  outside the installation names it relative to the object; a new
  FORMAT_VERSION; then 'installation' and 'source' leave product_facts
  (one line each) and KEY_FORMAT goes to 2.  Where the cache is used:
  Maker.make asks the plan before each step (Plan.restore, the step
  before the last, Cpp2So.restore) to place the cached products of the
  module, also before a step that would end the plan at its input, so
  the first import of a module under tiered places its object and loads
  the module compiled instead of interpreting it; Cpp2So.__call__ stores
  after a compile (Cpp2So.store).  sprite-make, the background compile of
  tiered (sprite-make --so in a child) and the prepare pass all go
  through Maker.  A module in the temporary directory of the system, or
  made by str2module, is never cached (excluded); SPRITE_FORCE_RECOMPILE_CXX
  skips the restore; a restore refuses a directory that cannot be
  written and a module whose import runs without an object.
  Concurrency: a store writes into a temporary directory beside the
  entry and renames it into place; a writer that finds the entry removes
  its own files; a restore places each file through a temporary name and
  a rename.  The shared object is hard-linked both ways when the cache
  and the product directory lie on one file system (Cpp2So.__call__
  unlinks the old object before the compiler runs, so a link into the
  cache is never written into; the generated file and the stamp are
  copied, because the emitter writes in place; a refused link falls back
  to a copy).  The restored files get modification times later than
  every file of the module, in the order of the chain, so the object is
  the newest file.  sprite-make prints "sprite-make: product cache: N
  restored, M stored" at the end of a run that did either, not under -q;
  its --jobs children print the line for the parent (--child), which
  sums them.  The prepare pass runs sprite-make without -q and reads the
  line from the log: the line of a directory says "43 of 43 from the
  product cache" and the summary line counts them.  The runner and
  cytest set SPRITE_PRODUCT_CACHE to tests/.cache/products unless the
  environment names it (the empty string included), beside the ICurry
  cache, so a test run writes nothing under the cache directory of the
  user and the restored objects are hard links.  scripts/new-worktree.sh
  no longer copies the C++ products of the main tree (stale in the
  worktree by the stamp and by the NEEDED entries, before this change as
  well); with SPRITE_WORKTREE_PREPARE=1 it ends with the prepare pass.
