  did not see is the step itself: the digest covers the sources of the
  pass, not the code that applies it (_frontend.curry2flat), so a change
  to the step alone (which files it rewrites, #99) left the key as it
  was.  The cache the main tree made on 10-07 after its refresh: 1,237
  of 1,441 rows reachable under HEAD, 7 with constrEq, the 7 modules the
  pass changes.
- The rule (_curry2icurry.translated_before_rewrite, asked by
  icurry_is_stale, which the plan asks of every .icy): an .icy is stale
  when the .fcy of the front end beside the source holds a Boolean
  equality the pass replaces and neither file holds constrEq.  The
  check reads the .fcy: constrEq in it means rewritten, no equality name
  means nothing to replace (both a fraction of a millisecond); else the
  pass runs over the text in memory and counts; the .icy is read only
  when the count is not zero, so an unrewritten .fcy the PAKCS oracle
  wrote beside an .icy of the rewritten program is current (the product
  is right; a stale verdict there would run the route at every import,
  since a hit of the ICurry cache writes the .icy and leaves the .fcy).
  Not judged: a .fcy older than the source (another version of the
  source, after a hit), and the modules of the library (committed
  ICurry, the owner's).  Cost: the Prelude 0.05 ms (a path comparison;
  its .fcy holds constrEq, the definition, so the first tier would
  answer for it too); the 893 .icy files under tests/data 0.74 s in
  all, 0.83 ms each (679 of the 880 .fcy files have an equality name
  without constrEq, so the parse runs for them: median 0.8 ms, mean
  0.9 ms, about 0.3 ms per KB, the largest 9 ms, assembler of kiel at
  28 KB).  Why not a marker time: a copy (tar
  -m in make overlay and in new-worktree.sh) gives a pre-rewrite pair a
  fresh time, so no time separates the two kinds of pair; the content
  does.  The product cache cannot bypass the verdict: Cpp2So.restore
  acts at the JSON and C++ stages alone, and its key digests the .icy
  text.
- The key: ROUTE_VERSION = 1 in _frontend.py, the version of the steps
  between the front end and the translation, in icurry_cache_key through
  cache.route_version(), not in frontend_digest: the product cache keys
  on that digest too and digests the ICurry text itself, so the version
  there would have recompiled every object once for nothing.  Every
  entry of the ICurry cache misses once after this change (one
  translation of each test module on the first run, as after the format
  change to 3); KEY_FORMAT stays 3, the row does not change.
- sprite-make prints "sprite-make: pre-rewrite pairs: N translated
  again" at the end of a run that made such a pair again (not under -q;
  the children of --jobs print it for the parent, which sums; the manual
  says so); the prepare pass reads the line (prepare.rewrite_counts) and
  repeats the count on the line of the directory and in its summary.
- Tests, both backends: tests/unit_curry2icurry_stale.py, 10 tests: the
  pair is stale and the import makes it again (the goal gives 3, the
  times move, a second make runs no front end); three current pairs,
  one per tier of the check, untouched by an import with the front end
  mocked away; the oracle's unrewritten file beside a rewritten .icy is
  current; a .fcy older than the source is not judged (the strict order
  of newer); the files not judged; the library left out; the cost (the
  best of five batches: the Prelude under 2 ms, a rewritten file under
  5 ms, a parse under 25 ms; measured 0.05, 0.1 and 1 ms); the version
  in the key and not in the digest; a version-0 entry misses, the route
  makes the pair again and stores the new entry, which a later miss is
  served from, and the old row stays; the lines of sprite-make (one
  pair, nothing on a second run and under -q, two children summed, the
  manual).  unit_runner.py: test_rewrite_counts (the line, the note, the
  summary).  tests/manifest.json has no entry for the new file.
