- Stage 5, curry.save, curry.load and the stepper on the C++ backend.
  Probes: curry.save writes C++ source (format 12, an IMPORTS line); the
  step of the toolchain that sprite-make --so runs
  (backends.cxx.toolchain.Cpp2So) compiles it into a shared object when
  the file lies under the product directory of its source
  (_filenames.curryfilename refuses another path, "bad path for"); and
  curry.load reads the object.  For a module whose library is not loaded
  (hello of the pool, interpreted; a fresh module under interpret:new)
  the load returns the module in the same session, again after
  curry.reset(), and in a fresh Interpreter.  For a module loaded from its
  installed object (Data.Either) the loader refuses the file in all three
  cases ("it is already loaded from .../Data/.curry/sprite-pakcs-3.4.1/
  Either.so"): the runtime keeps one library per module name for the life
  of the process.  The saved program of the C++ backend is a stub:
  _generate_main writes an entry() that prints "Entry for 'M'" and exits.
  The stepper: cytest.step calls Evaluator.set_global_step_limit, which
  reads rts.stepcounter, and the C++ RuntimeState has none
  (AttributeError).  The C++ runtime counts the nested steps through
  procS (check_interrupts increments stepcount), so a step limit would be
  a field of the RuntimeState checked in check_interrupts with a
  terminate status that procD hands out: a change of the runtime,
  proposed, not made.  evaluator.single_step takes one step at the root on
  both backends (one step of goal gives f (g (g 0))).  Tests:
  unit_loadsave.py runs on both backends.  LoadSaveTestCase saves the four
  library modules in the form of the backend and loads the file: a known
  failure on the C++ backend (curry.load reads a .so), so its four tests
  are expected failures there.  TestRoundTrip (new) runs the round trip of
  each backend in a child: the .py form on the Python backend; on the C++
  backend the C++ source compiled by Cpp2So under the product directory
  and loaded with curry.load, in the session and after a reset, under
  interpret:new so that the fresh module stays interpreted (skipped
  without a C++ compiler).  TestSave of unit_goals.py runs on both
  backends: the four tests that run the saved program are expected
  failures on the C++ backend (SAVED_PROGRAM), test_library checks both
  forms (no moduleMain, no entry()), test_goal_from_scheme runs on both.
  test_interp_step of unit_py_runtime.py and test_apply_nf of
  unit_prelude.py are expected failures on the C++ backend instead of
  skips.  cytest.expectedFailureIf(condition, reason) is the decorator
  (tests/lib/cytest/__init__.py; a conditional expectedFailure, so a fix
  shows as an unexpected success).  The decision on a C++ form of
  curry.save (a main that evaluates the goal; a plan step that compiles a
  saved file) and on a step limit of the runtime stays with the owner;
  example 04 and the Python form follow it.
- Finding (a) of the audit: Interpreter(flags) started from the defaults.
  Probe: with SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS=backend:py,inline_budget:7 and
  SPRITE_ROTATION=steps:100 the global interpreter had py 7 steps:100 and
  Interpreter() had cxx 4 time:10ms.  The rule now: the flags of every
  interpreter start from the environment (flags.getflags: the defaults,
  SPRITE_ROTATION, SPRITE_INTERPRETER_FLAGS, the argument on top), as the
  global interpreter and curry.reload did already; Interpreter.__new__
  merges getflags(flags) over the defaults.  So a fresh interpreter of a
  test runs on the backend and in the mode of the session; test_04_cxx of
  unit_plan.py pins interpret:off, because under all the skeleton of the
  C++ backend ends at the JSON.  The docstrings of curry.interpreter.flags
  and of getflags state the rule; CurryPackage.rst and the note of
  UsingTheAPI.rst say that a new interpreter starts from the environment.
  TestFreshInterpreterFlags of unit_api.py pins it in a child: the
  environment is the base, the argument wins, an unknown flag is refused.
- Finding (b) of the audit: a cyrt Node compared with a Python value.
  Probe on the C++ backend: == and != against 1, True, None, object(),
  'a', 1.0 and [] raised TypeError (pybind11: incompatible function
  arguments), in both directions and in a containment test; on the Python
  backend == gave False for the unboxed primitives and raised TypeError
  ("not a Curry expression") for None, object() and [].  Fix: the bindings
  define __eq__ and __ne__ with py::is_operator(), so a failed cast returns
  NotImplemented and Python answers False and True; the Node of the Python
  backend returns NotImplemented for a value that is not a Curry
  expression.  Both backends answer False for == and True for != against
  every foreign object, in both directions and in a containment test.
  TestForeignObjects of unit_equality.py pins it on both backends.  Hash
  and ordering: neither backend defines an order (TypeError from Python
  itself, no gap); both hash by identity (Node::hash is std::hash of the
  pointer; the Python Node hashes id(self)), so two logically equal nodes
  hash differently and a set of two equal nodes has size 2 on both
  backends: a gap against the contract of __hash__, reported, not fixed
  (the test pins hash(node) == hash(node) alone).
