2026-10-08: compiled objects portable between trees (format 13; worktree wt/objfmt; the decision of 2026-10-08 under issue #100)
- The decision.  An object of the C++ backend names no path, so an object
  compiled in one tree serves another: a copy of the tree at another
  path, a relocated installation, and the product cache of a second
  worktree.  The link line, the stamp, the cache key and the loader
  changed; the runtime (node layout, info tables, bom.hpp) did not.
- The SONAME.  Every object carries the SONAME "sprite-<module>.so.<format>"
  (toolchain.soname; module_of_soname is the inverse): the full module
  name, dots included, and compiler.FORMAT_VERSION.  The first form tried,
  "<module>.so.<format>", matched the names of the system libraries
  (libgcc_s.so.1 parsed as the module libgcc_s, and the stage failed with
  "Curry module 'libgcc_s' not found"); the prefix holds a character no
  module name holds, so the two kinds of name never meet.  The compile
  step reads the module name from a new header line of the generated file,
  "// MODULE: N", after the IMPORTS and FORMAT lines (vEmitHeader; a file
  of the current format without the line is a PrerequisiteError), and
  passes -Wl,-soname.  The objects of the imports stand on the link line
  by their files as before; the linker records their SONAMEs as the NEEDED
  entries, so readelf -d of every installed object shows no path
  (sprite-Prelude.so.13, sprite-Data.Maybe.so.13, libcyrt.so and the
  system libraries).  The link line pins -Wl,--no-as-needed (LINK_FLAGS,
  after the review below), so the NEEDED entries name every import of the
  module in the order of the IMPORTS line, whatever the default of the
  toolchain; the imports of a module stay in its record as well.
- The loader.  The dynamic linker satisfies a NEEDED entry with an object
  of that SONAME the process has mapped, whatever its path, RTLD_LOCAL or
  RTLD_GLOBAL, and no search path leads to the file (the probe: two small
  objects outside Sprite, A.so with a SONAME and B.so linked against it;
  B alone fails with "ProbeA.so.13: cannot open shared object file", A
  then B gives 42 under both modes, A from another directory then B too,
  and a directory on LD_LIBRARY_PATH that holds a link named like the
  SONAME serves as well; glibc 2.39).  So loader.load_module reads the
  NEEDED entries of the object before it opens it (backends/cxx/elf.py, a
  reader of the dynamic section of a 64-bit ELF file, checked against
  readelf in the tests), imports the modules they name
  (loader.needed_modules), and raises DynloadError when one of them runs
  without an object ("its import X runs without a compiled object")
  instead of the message of the dynamic linker.  The plan path imported
  them already (Cpp2So.import_lacks_an_object, from the IMPORTS line);
  the loader's own pass covers curry.load and an object without its
  generated file.  The tiered swap (cyrt/tiered.cpp) is unchanged: a job
  runs after the jobs of its imports and the swap maps the import's
  object before the importer's; a failed import swap now leaves the
  importer interpreted with "cannot open shared object file" in the log,
  where the path-based entry mapped the import's file from disk.  A
  program that dlopens an object without the loader must open the objects
  of its imports first, in dependency order, with libcyrt.so loaded or on
  the search path, or name on LD_LIBRARY_PATH a directory that holds each
  import under its SONAME (Cpp2So documents it).  bin/sprite-invoke
  exports LD_LIBRARY_PATH=$SPRITE_HOME/lib, which is why the extension
  module and the objects find libcyrt.so by name; a test of a relocated
  copy must set the variable to the copy, as the launcher of the copy
  would.
- The record.  compiler._source_file_name names a source under the
  installation relative to it, as before, and any other source relative
  to the directory where the toolchain places the object of that source,
  "../../M.curry" (the product directory beside the source).  The reader,
  loader.source_file, tells the forms apart by the prefix: a record that
  begins with "../" or "./" is relative to the directory of the object,
  another relative record is relative to the installation, an absolute
  record is a path.  The C++ side stores the string and reads it nowhere
  (dynload.cpp); the one other reader, unit_cxx_bom.py, checks the suffix.
  A module from text (curry.compile, str2module) keeps its temporary
  directory in the metadata entry all.tmpd, as before; such a module is
  never cached.  The record of the Python backend is unchanged (an
  absolute path for a source outside the installation): the backend has
  no objects and writes its files again in a moment.
- The format and the key.  compiler.FORMAT_VERSION is 13; the digest of
  the stamp covers it (object_digest, "format %d"; TestFlavor.test_digest
  asserts it), so every object was compiled once more: make stage
  recompiled the 13 library objects, and the prepare pass of the pool (43
  modules) took 35.5 s cold.  Cpp2So.product_facts dropped 'installation'
  and 'source'; nothing else in the key names a tree (the generator digest
  and cache.frontend_digest digest contents, 'subdir' is a name, the chain
  digests the texts of the module and of its imports, and the .icy and
  the JSON of the library hold no path), and _productcache.KEY_FORMAT is 3
  (the parts of the key changed).  Measured: a second tree made of the
  pool (tests/data/curry copied without its C++ products, under the build
  root, outside TMPDIR, which the cache excludes) with the cache of the
  runner: sprite-make -k -c -z --so over the 43 modules in 0.7 s,
  "product cache: 43 restored, 0 stored", the restored object one inode
  with the first tree's, its record "../../CxxBom.curry", and CxxBom
  imported from the copy in a child with the compiler counted: the value
  9, no command, compile 0.0 s.  The first attempt under TMPDIR compiled
  all 43 in 32 s: _productcache.excluded leaves the temporary directory
  of the system out, by design.
- Tests.  unit_cxx_toolchain.py (42 on cxx; the TestPortableObjects
  class): the SONAME form and the names that are not one; the SONAME and
  the NEEDED entries of a compiled module, read by elf.read_dynamic and
  checked against readelf, no path in any entry, the MODULE line and the
  record "../../M.curry" in the generated file, no path of the tree in it,
  and the module object that names the source beside the product
  directory; the two forms of the record and their resolution; every
  current object of the installed library (SONAME of its module, no path,
