  so two equal nodes hash differently and a set keyed by nodes keeps
  both, a node is not meant as a dictionary key, a node has no order;
  with a transcript.  No page described the equality against a foreign
  object before; the lane of #82 stage 5 documented it in the source
  and in unit_equality.py alone.  test_hash of unit_equality.py pins
  the rule: node == same, hash(node) != hash(same), len({node, same})
  == 2, same not in {node}, {node: 1}.get(same) is None, both backends.
