2026-10-08: #102 under interpret:off and the step limit of the C++ runtime (decisions 5 and 6b of the action list; worktree wt/potholes3; the entry of the toolchain item of this lane precedes this one)
- #102, reproduced: under interpret:off a module imported from an ICurry
  object (the ICurry of CxxInterp read from its JSON, then
  curry.import_(imodule)) got its functions without a step (has_step
  False), and the first evaluation, fact 3, died with SIGSEGV in
  generate_values; tiered and new gave 6.
- The fix, two lines of defence.  (1) The compile on first use.  Under
  'off' a function table made at run time gets the trap step of the
  runtime (cyrt/module.hpp: trap_step, install_trap, is_trapped,
  set_trap_hook, owner_of; backends/cxx/materialize.py: Materializer.trap
  and one Pending entry per module, with the search path of the import
  from module_loaded).  The first call of such a function calls the hook
  (materialize.first_use), which compiles the module in the process: its
  pending imports first; the tables kept for the process and the shim of
  the module linked and loaded (tiered._register_shim, _load_shim), so
  that the object binds its tables to the tables the nodes and the
  Python objects hold; the C++ of the ICurry object in hand written with
  interp.save beside the source (.curry/<subdir>/M.cpp; a directory of
  the process when the module has no source file or the directory
  cannot be written), compiled by Cpp2So, the step sprite-make --so runs
  (the product cache keeps the object); and the object loaded with the
  swap of tiered execution on the calling thread (cyrt.tiered_adopt;
  Module::adopt takes a trapped table as it takes an interpreted one).
  Then the trap runs the compiled step on the same redex, and cxx.shlib
  names the object.  Why at the first call and not at the import: the
  plan imports every module it compiles the same way (Json2TargetSource,
  which generates code and evaluates nothing), and a compile at the
  import would recurse; the trap fires on a call alone.  (2) The error.
  A function whose table still has the trap when it is called, because
  the hook refused (the test hook materialize.COMPILE_ON_FIRST_USE is
  False), the compile failed, or the module is gone, sets the error of
  its configuration and returns E_ERROR, so the evaluation raises
  EvaluationError "cannot evaluate function 'M.f': the function has no
  compiled code (its module was imported from an ICurry object under
  interpret:off): <reason>" (the module from owner_of, a scan of the
  loaded modules).  The hook runs inside the step, so the trampoline of
  the bindings reports an exception of the hook as the reason; an
  interrupt and an exit pass.  RuntimeState.single_step raises the
  error a step reports (it dropped it), through the Python wrapper that
  turns the exception of the bindings into curry.exceptions.EvaluationError.
  has_step is False for a trapped table, and icurry_attach accepts one.
- The step limit (6b).  RuntimeState::step_limit (NOLIMIT by default,
  cyrt/state/rts.hpp); procS returns the new status E_TERMINATE (-16,
  fwd.hpp) after the completed step that brings steps_total to the
  limit (the test reads the count before the increment: one compare per
  step); procN hands it out with E_UNWIND and the others, hnf and the
  generated code return it as any status below T_CTOR, a nested procD
  yields it to the enclosing evaluation, and the outermost procD throws
  StepLimitReached (exceptions.hpp, bound in the bindings), which
  generate_values turns into control.E_TERMINATE.  So the graph holds
  the result of exactly N steps, no redex is half rewritten, and the
  counters of the rotation do not move (stepcount is not touched;
  unit_cxx_rotation.py pins 16929/1636 and 2112920/511484 as before).
  The interface: the Python wrapper of the state has stepcounter, a view
  (cyrtbindings.StepCounter) with global_count, global_limit and
  reset_global over steps_total, step_limit and _step_base of the state,
  so Evaluator.set_global_step_limit, evaluator.evaluate(..., steplimit=N)
  and cytest.step work unchanged on both backends; a limit at or below
  the count raises at once, as on the Python backend.  The backends give
  the same states (f (g (g 0)): f (g 0) after 2 steps, f 0 after 3; a
  goal of 29 steps: E_TERMINATE at 28 and 29, the value at 30), so
  test_interp_step of unit_py_runtime.py and test_apply_nf of
  unit_prelude.py pass on the C++ backend and their expectedFailureIf
  decorators are gone.  A view and not a field, because a counter held
  by the state made a reference cycle: the state of
  evaluator.single_step (one per compiled expression) then lived until
  the cyclic collector ran, a live state is a root of the C++ collector,
  and the history of a search stayed reachable, so
  test_repl_eval_under_stress of unit_goals.py (last of 8000 elements
  under SPRITE_GC_STRESS=1) grew with the square of the length (1000
  elements 5.0 s, 2000 16.0 s, 4000 46.6 s, 8000 past its bound of 60 s)
  and takes 1.0 s for 4000 again; test_counter_keeps_no_cycle of
  unit_cxx_steplimit.py pins it.
- A finding on the way, fixed: the second half of test_apply_nf
  evaluates id $!! (x::Int) where x free.  The C++ runtime suspended
  (hnf of the free variable at the root of the argument records a
  residual, which applynf_step returned); the Python backend and PAKCS
  3.4.1 give the variable, _a.  applynf_step now takes the residual back,
  as hnf_or_free does, and reports T_FREE, which _applyspecial accepts,
  so ($!!) and normalForm give the variable, and ($##), which needs a
  ground normal form, suspends as before (applygnf_step).
  test_apply_nf_free_variable of unit_prelude.py pins the three on both
  backends.  A change of a primitive beyond the items, made because the
  test the owner asked to flip needs it; apply.cpp, twelve lines.
