2026-10-08: the unboxed marker outside a primitive, and the walk over shared constants (#107, #108; worktree wt/apibugs)
- #107.  curry.unboxed is the marker of the payload of a primitive,
  [Prelude.Int, curry.unboxed(3)].  Anywhere else the typed builder gave
  the marker the type of its payload and handed the raw Python value to
  make_node as a successor, and the untyped builder returned the raw value
  from its handler: curry.expr(P.id, curry.unboxed(1)),
  curry.raw_expr(P.id, curry.unboxed(1)) and curry.expr([curry.unboxed(1)])
  ended the process on the C++ backend (exit 139; the bindings dereferenced
  the value as a node), the Python backend built the ill-formed node
  <id 1>, and the marker alone returned the Python int.  Both builders
  raise a CurryTypeError now, on both backends.  The untyped builder
  (expressions.ExpressionBuilder) takes the marker under a primitive in its
  symbol handler, make_node(Int, 3), and its handler of the marker raises
  for every other place; the typed builder keeps the Unboxed spec, so a
  description prints it, and TypedProblem._enter raises a ConversionError
  of the catalogue with the position.  The message names the marker and
  its place: "curry.unboxed(1) stands at argument 1 of Prelude.id :: a ->
  a; the marker is the payload of an Int, Char or Float, as in
  [Prelude.Int, curry.unboxed(1)]"; the marker alone "stands alone"; in
  the untyped builder, which has no positions, it "stands outside a
  primitive" (expressions.unboxed_message).  The ValueError "cannot
  rewrite a node to an unboxed value" is gone: the marker alone with a
  target is the same CurryTypeError, and the target stays.  Tests:
  TestUnboxedMarker of unit_expr.py (the payload through both builders;
  alone, as an argument, in a list and in a tuple through expr,
  describe().typeof(), eval, raw_expr and untyped_expr; the message names
  the primitive of the payload; the error carries where and value; a
  target stays).  The docs: Using the API (the boxed and unboxed values,
  the list of the markers) and The curry Package.
  Review findings applied.  A payload of the wrong Python type under a
  primitive built garbage: [Float, unboxed(1)] was <Float 5.0e-324> on
  the C++ backend (the bits of the int as a double) and <Float 1> on the
  Python backend; [Int, unboxed(2.5)], [Int, unboxed('a')], [Char,
  unboxed(1)] and [Int, unboxed(True)] disagreed between the backends the
  same way, through both builders.  Both builders check the payload
  before make_node now (expressions.unboxed_fits, unboxed_payload): a
  Python int under Int (not a bool), a str of length one under Char, a
  float under Float.  The typed builder raises a ConversionError at the
  position of the primitive, "curry.unboxed(2.5) is not the payload of an
  Int at argument 1 of Prelude.id :: a -> a; the payload of an Int is a
  Python int, as in [Prelude.Int, curry.unboxed(3)]"; the untyped builder
  raises the CurryTypeError without the position.  The range of Int is
  checked where the node is made, as before (#105).  The marker has a
  repr, curry.unboxed(1), so the EvaluationError of an item of an
  iterator (converted when the list is demanded) names it instead of the
  default object repr.  Tests: test_payload_type and test_iterator of
  TestUnboxedMarker, both backends.
- Files outside the item that the decision touched, reported here.  The
  runtime tests built Choice nodes as [Choice, unboxed(cid), l, r] and
  heterogeneous tuples through raw_expr, which the decision refuses.
  tests/unit_py_interp.py and tests/unit_py_runtime.py build the choice
  through the choice marker, choice(cid, l, r), the same raw node
  (bootstrap.cid.value is the id; tests/lib/cytest/bootstrap.py keeps the
  marker).  tests/lib/cytest/expression_library.py pairs the variable of
  the value binding with a boxed 2, the shape make_value_bindings of
  rts_bindings.py makes, so test_equals_value_binding of unit_equality.py
  runs on the C++ backend too (its skip said the pair was heterogeneous).
  unit_equality.py maps an unboxed marker to its bare value itself (raw)
  for the rows that compare unboxed values; unit_indexing.py indexes the
  bare values in test_index_unboxed; unit_py_conversions.py asserts the
  error; unit_py_copy.py copies a Choice node, which holds its id unboxed;
  unit_typed_expr.py asserts the error.
- #108.  A value that held the empty list at two types could not be typed
  on the C++ backend: curry.typeof(v) for v from [[1, 2], [3]],
  ['ab', 'c'] or ([1], 'ab') raised MismatchError, and curry.eval(P.unwords,
  strs) with such a value failed the same way; the Python backend typed
  them.  The C++ runtime has one [] node (builtins.hpp: Nil, with Unit,
  True, False, Fail, the small ints and chars, and the partial_node of a
  module); the List spec and string_node of the typed builder make the
  empty list through make_node(Nil), which returns that node; and the walk
  of TypedProblem._walk memoized by node id, so one type variable served
  every position of [].  The walk keeps the memo for the nodes with
  successors and the free variables (a shared subgraph is visited once, a
  cycle ends, a variable has one type) and types a node without node
  successors at every position it holds, with a fresh instance of its
  scheme (builder._constant: a nullary constructor, a partial application
  without arguments, a function without arguments, a failure; not a
  primitive, a string or a generator).  Chosen over the other two shapes:
  a position key for "a nullary constructor shared across types" needs the
  types before the memo decides, and the walk meets the positions one at a
  time; a fresh [] node for every empty list is a change to the runtime,
  the item of the C++ backend, and would not reach the values compiled
  code makes.  The change is sound because such a node carries no type of
  its own: the sharing is an artifact of the runtime, and a fresh instance
  of the scheme at each position is what the front end gave each
  occurrence of the constant in the source.  Every position counts towards
  VALUE_WALK_CAP, so a value of n empty lists counts 2n + 1 nodes on both
  backends, as it did on the Python backend before.  After review the
  walk reads the successors of a node once: a child is pushed with a
  fresh variable, the visit decides from the successors it reads anyway
  whether the node enters the memo, and a shared node pushed twice before
  its first visit unifies with the memo and is not counted again (the
  helper _constant, which read the successors of every child once more,
  is gone).  Measured cost, curry.typeof of a value of the evaluator,
  best of three, the other lane on the machine: C++ backend, a list of
  30000 Ints 1.054 s with the first shape of the walk and 0.888 s after
  (the old memo gave 0.883 s in the review); 30000 empty lists 1.444 s
  and 1.177 s; 15000 (Int, []) pairs 2.118 s and 1.474 s (the old memo
  raised MismatchError on both).  Python backend, 8000 elements: Ints
  0.188 s and 0.175 s (the old memo 0.178 s); empty lists 0.230 s and
  0.220 s (0.129 s); pairs 0.248 s and 0.228 s (0.163 s).  The cost that
  remains on constant-dense values is the typing of each position.  Both
  backends give
  the same answers now: ([], []) is ([a], [b]), (failed, failed) is (a, b),
  (id, id) is (a -> a, b -> b), and a shared Just [] held twice is
  (Maybe [a], Maybe [a]).  Tests: TestSharedConstants of unit_typecheck.py
  (the three values typed and passed to unwords, snd, fst and concat; the
  value of words "ab c"; the constants per position; a shared non-empty
  node, a cycle and a free variable typed once; the cap counts positions
  and a shared cell once).  The docs: Using the API, the values of earlier
  evaluations; the cap is stated in positions there and in the Limits
  (a shared [] counts once per position), since the error text "more
  than 100000 nodes" counts positions on the C++ backend.
- Not fixed, the owner's call (beyond the risk line).  The record of result
  types (builder.TypeRecord) is keyed by the identity of the node, and the
  C++ backend returns the one [] node from curry.expr([]), curry.expr('')
  and curry.expr([], exprtype='[Int]') alike, so the last call wins: after
  e1 = curry.expr([], exprtype='[Int]') and e2 = curry.expr(''),
  curry.typeof(e1) is [Char] on the C++ backend ([Int] on the Python
  backend, where the nodes differ), and the value [] of
  curry.eval([P.filter, P.odd, [2, 4]]) types as [Char] after a
  curry.expr('') in the same interpreter ([a] on the Python backend).
  The conversions of curry.eval are affected too when a value of the
  evaluator is the bare empty list (observed on the C++ backend, the
  Python backend gives the other answers): with v the value of
  curry.eval([P.filter, P.odd, [2, 4]]) and one curry.expr('') after it,
  curry.eval(P.Cons, 1, v) raises MismatchError ("argument 2 has type
  Char") where it gave [[1]] before the curry.expr(''), curry.eval([[1,
  2], v]) raises the same, and curry.eval((v, 0)) yields ('', 0) because
  the walk takes the record of the shared node through its shortcut and
  the converter prints the empty list as a string; after a
  curry.expr([], exprtype='[Bool]'), the value w of curry.eval([P.words,
  '']) fails curry.eval(P.unwords, w).  One curry.expr('') earlier in the
  life of the interpreter is enough: the entry lives as long as the
  shared node.  This is the symptom of #108 for a value that is the empty
  list rather than one that holds it.  The builder alone cannot make the
  record sound: a record skipped for such a node makes curry.eval('')
  yield [] instead of '' on both backends, because the converter reads the
  record.  A mitigation inside the builder, not applied: the walk skips
  the shortcut of the record when the root has no node successors, so a
  bare constant value is typed by its scheme; its visible cost is that
  curry.eval(P.id, curry.expr('')) prints [] on both backends.  The full
  proposals: the evaluator takes the goal's type from the goal object
  instead of the record, and the record skips the nodes the runtime
  shares; or make_node of the C++ bindings returns a fresh [] node to
  Python (the layout stays; the shared Nil of compiled code is untouched).
  The small ints raise the same question in a weaker form: curry.expr(5)
  and curry.expr(5, exprtype='Int') are one node, whose record says
  Num a => a or Int by the last call.  The catalogue (P4) also asks
  whether curry.unboxed should be a public marker at all; nothing in the
  API needs it, since a Python value builds the same node.
