  The forward node: <_Fwd <Int 2>> is the form of the example;
  <_FwdSz <O> 24> is the form for a constructor value (Peano.add O O).
  The long added sentences of the pages, the docstrings, the example
  README and tests/README were split, one fact each.  After the review:
  func_docs.py and unit_reference_backend.py 4 of 4 ok on both backends;
  make -C docs html 0 warnings.

2026-10-08: the refused load, the load during the background compile, and the edited source (#109, #110; worktree wt/apibugs2)
- #109, path A, the refused load.  Reproduced (the case D of the API
  study: curry.load(soA), the refused curry.load(soB) of a second object of
  module M, then curry.eval(M.main)): exit 139.  gdb: the fault is in
  cyrt::pack under Node::create, which reads info->format of a table of M.
  The cause, confirmed by the trace: the loader opened the second library
  before it compared the registered file.  The dynamic linker binds the
  symbols of a library to the first definition in the global scope, and a
  compiled module is linked with -Bsymbolic-functions, which binds its
  functions and not its data, so the second library's references to its
  own tables resolved to the tables of the first library; InfoTable has a
  constructor, so the tables are dynamic initializers (.init_array), and
  the load of the second library constructed its tables at the addresses
  of the first library's tables, with name, format and step pointers into
  the second library.  The refusal dropped the second library (the
  loader's SharedCurryModule was garbage), dlclose unmapped it (a compiled
  module defines no STB_GNU_UNIQUE symbol, so nothing marks it NODELETE),
  and the tables of the loaded module pointed into unmapped memory.  The
  cases I and J of the study passed here before the change; they are the
  same mechanism with a reset between (a reset does not rewrite the tables
  of a module loaded from its object), and the fix covers them.  Fix: the
  decision is taken before any dlopen.  The module name comes from the
  SONAME of the object (elf.read_dynamic, which the loader read already
  for the NEEDED entries; toolchain.module_of_soname), and the registry of
  the runtime is asked by that name through a new binding,
  SharedCurryModule.find (the registry is the truth: a library stays
  registered after a reset while its tables are kept, where
  interp.modules and the Module of the runtime know nothing of it).  A
  different file is refused with the documented message, and nothing was
  opened.  The compare after the open stays as the backstop for an object
  without the SONAME of its module (one written before format 13), whose
  name is known only after the open; such an object is still opened before
  the compare, as before.  No other route was smaller: the bill of
  materials of the file needs the relocations applied to read its name,
  and an open with RTLD_LOCAL binds the references of the object to the
  global scope all the same.
- #109, path B, the load during the background compile.  Reproduced (the
  probe of P55: Peano.curry newer than Peano.so, from curry.lib import
  Peano, curry.load of the object, then the exit; also with curry.save
  before the load): "exiting normally" printed, exit 139.  The trace.
  gdb: __strlen_evex under cyrt::Module::clear() under Module::~Module,
  called from the pybind11 deallocator during the garbage collection of
  the exit of Python (Py_RunMain, _Py_Dealloc).  Module::clear keeps the
  tables of an interpreted module and reads info->name to key them; the
  name pointed into unmapped memory.  LD_DEBUG=files: the loader opened
  Peano.so ("dynamically loaded by libcyrt.so"), the shim of Peano was
  "needed by Peano.so (relocation dependency)", so the object bound its
  tables to the live tables of the interpreted module, and "calling fini"
  and "destroying link map" of Peano.so followed at once: import_ returned
  the module of the import, nothing kept the loader's SharedCurryModule,
  dlclose unmapped the object, and the live tables kept the name, format
  and step pointers the initializers of the object had written.  So the
  place is the drop of the library inside curry.load, not the child
  replacing the mapped file (Cpp2So unlinks the old object and the linker
  writes a new inode, so a mapped object stays), and not the order of the
  exit handlers (the exit only reads what dangled already).  A use of the
  module right after the load crashes at once in Node::create (the
  variant with an evaluation), so the hazard does not need the exit; and
  tiered.wait before the load exits 0 (the swap came first, and the load
  is then the benign same-file case: the registry names the file, the
  second dlopen returns the mapped object, no initializer runs).  Nor does
  the hazard need a running compile: under tiered any curry.load of the
  object of a module that runs interpreted binds through the shim the
  same way, whether its compile failed, was blocked, or never ran for the
  lack of a compiler.  Decision: the wait.  When a compile of the module
  is queued, running or finished and not applied (tiered.pending, a record
  the Python side keeps from submit to poll or cancel), the loader waits
  for that module (tiered.wait_for: the compiles queued before it end
  first, the worker is FIFO) and logs one line at the INFO level; the swap
  then loads the object the compile wrote, the module is loaded from its
  object, and the load proceeds as the same-file case.  The wait is sound
  because after the swap the object is kept by the Module of the runtime
  and the registry names it; a refusal would have made the sequence of the
  Quickstart (save, sprite-make --so, load) an error whenever the import
  had started a compile.  The sibling case the trace exposed, a module that
  stays interpreted with no compile pending (its compile failed, or no
  compiler), is refused before the open: "the module runs, or ran,
  interpreted in this process; load the object in a new process" (the
  wording and the scope after the review, below).  A load
  of such an object would need the swap itself (the shims, the link to the
  Module, the check that the object is the translation of the ICurry in
  memory: the route of the trap step in materialize.compile_pending), which
  is a design of the loader and the owner's.  curry.load of an object of a
  module that runs interpreted under interpret:new keeps its old meaning
  (no shim, the object's own storage, the loaded module comes back): the
  round trip of unit_loadsave.py relies on it.  The wait has no timeout; a
  Ctrl-C reaches it between the slices of a quarter second.
- #110, cause (a), the edited source.  import_ of a loaded name returns
  the loaded module and now logs a warning that names the module and the
  file and says that a new process reads the edited file; the REPL's :load
  logs it at every :load (commands.load calls warn_edited with always=True
  before import_, which then says nothing more for that edit).  The notice
  is the module logger at the WARNING level, the convention of the front
  end's warning on overlapping rules and of the notices of tiered
  execution; not the warnings module.  import_ logs it once per edit of a
  module (a memo keyed by the name, the file and its modification time),
  because every import of a module that imports M calls import_(M), and
  the expression module of every :eval imports the loaded module: without
  the memo one edit would print a line at every evaluation.  The check:
  the import records the modification time of the source (st_mtime_ns;
  Handle.source_mtime, set by record_source inside ImportEx), and the
  check compares it with the file.  Not the products' times, as the item
  suggested: the child of the background compile makes the products again
  from the edited source (its front end runs when the source is newer than
  the .icy), so after it the products are newer than the source while the
  module in memory is the one of the first text (seen in the probe of
  cause (b)); the product times then say "current" exactly where the
  warning is due.  A module without a source file, or whose file is gone
  (a module compiled from a string), records nothing and is never stale.
  The message says a new process, not the REPL's restart: :set backend
  reloads the interpreter and loads the module again while repl.module
  still holds the old module object, and on the C++ backend a module of
  the same name takes its tables back with their steps while such an
  object is alive (P30), so the REPL has no restart short of a new
  process; the REPL page says quit and start again.
- #110, cause (b), the swap.  Chosen: the check at poll.  A job carries
  the digest of the ICurry file (.icy) of the module at the import (size
  and CRC-32, tiered_file_digest: one implementation in the runtime, which
  the Python side calls at submit; the JSON at first, changed after the
  review, below), and apply (cyrt/tiered.cpp) refuses the object when the
  digest of the file differs at the swap, after the shims are loaded and
  before the dlopen; the result carries the text TIERED_EDITED, poll logs
  a warning, and the module stays interpreted with the code the import
  read.  The child cannot be told to compile from the products in
  hand: sprite-make takes a module or a source, and the plan starts from
  the newest file of the chain, which an edit makes the source; a new
  option of sprite-make is the owner's.  A refused swap counts as a failed
  module in tiered.status and in failed_compiles of curry.stats.  The two
  fields of TieredJob changed tiered.hpp, a runtime header under
  include/cyrt, so the ABI stamp changed and every object compiled before
  is stale: make stage compiled the library again (48 s), and the prepare
  pass compiled the pool of the tests (101 modules in two directories,
  72 s).
