- Assessed, left to the owner (beyond the risk line).  A true reload of a
  module in one process: the tables of an interpreted module live for the
  process and a module of the same name takes them back with their steps
  while a module object is alive (P30), the registry keeps one library per
  name, the bytecode of other modules and compiled objects name the tables
  by address, and live expressions hold nodes of those tables; a reload
  that gives the dependents, the expressions and the registry a sound
  meaning is a design of the runtime.  A :reload command of the REPL (as
  PAKCS has) needs that reload, or a re-exec of the process.  A stamp or a
  record that names the digest of the JSON the object was compiled from
  would let the loader verify an object against the ICurry in memory and
  would make the adopt route of curry.load possible for an interpreted
  module; that is a change to the object format or the stamp.  The
  backstop for an object without a module SONAME opens before the compare,
  as before.  The refusal of the object of a module that stays interpreted
  is a new refusal in the shape the issue decided, named here for the
  owner's eye.
