- The review of the change (the same day), the findings applied.
  (1) The digest of the swap was of the .json.z the import read.  The
  child's sprite-make --so writes the JSON in the plain form and the
  import's plan in the compact one, so a touch of the source, or an edit
  of a comment, made the child run the front end again and refused the
  swap (TIERED_EDITED) for a program that did not change, and the module
  lost its compiled tier for the session.  The digest is of the .icy now,
  which the front end writes byte for byte the same for the same program
  (the port and icurry agree, tests/README); the fields of TieredJob are
  named icurryfile and icurry_digest (a header change, so the ABI stamp
  changed once more and the library and the pool were compiled again).
  Test: unit_reload.py, test_edit_of_a_comment_keeps_the_swap.
  (2) The wait before a load skipped when the registry named the kept
  library of an earlier incarnation (after a reset the same path is
  registered while the second incarnation runs interpreted with a compile
  pending): the loader waits whenever a compile of the name is pending,
  before it asks the registry.  Test: unit_cxx_tiered.py,
  test_load_waits_after_a_reset.
  (3) A :load, or an import_ with is_sourcefile, of another file of the
  same module name returned the loaded module and said nothing: a warning
  names both files now (import_.warn_other_file; commands.load passes the
  file the argument names, or the .curry beside a bare module name).
  Test: unit_reload.py, test_load_of_another_file_warns.
  (4) The child of test_quickstart_sequence_twice ran the foreground
  sprite-make --so while the background compile of the import wrote the
  same M.cpp and M.so: a race (a torn M.cpp fails one compile, and a failed
  background compile turns the load into a refusal).  The child waits for
  the background compile before sprite-make, as a session at the keyboard
  does; the load during the compile is its own test.  The Quickstart says
  to run sprite-make once the background compile ended.  A lock on the
  products directory, so that two compiles of one module never write at
  once, is the owner's.
  (5) The dead case 'K' of TestRefusedLoad.run_case is a test now: the
  same file loaded twice, then a use.
  (6) loader.module_name had no caller: removed (_dynamic_names stays).
  (7) The refusal of the object of a module that runs interpreted was
  gated on the name in interp.modules.  After a reset the name is gone
  while the tables of the interpreted module are kept for the process and
  its shim names them; a load then accepted bound the object to the kept
  tables (confirmed here: the probe of the review, M imported stale, the
  compile cancelled, a reset, curry.load accepted, then curry.eval of
  M.main dumped core at once, status 139; the review saw the crash one
  step later, at the import after a second reset, once the new module had
  released the object).  The loader refuses whenever a shim names the
  tables of the name and no library is registered under it, in or out of
  interp.modules: "the module runs, or ran, interpreted in this process;
  load the object in a new process".  The case "swapped, reset, load of
  the same file" is unchanged: the registry answers first.  The plan's
  import of a current object after such a reset meets the same refusal,
  as a DynloadError of the import; the alternative was the crash at the
  next reset.  Test: unit_cxx_tiered.py, test_reset_then_load_is_refused
  (a child process, status 0, the module imports and runs after the
  refusal).
  (8) The wait of curry.load was announced at the INFO level alone, under
  a default level of WARNING: after five seconds (loader.WAIT_NOTICE_SECONDS)
  one line at the WARNING level names the module and the object.  A bound
  on the wait, after which the load refuses, is a new default and the
  owner's; the wait runs in slices of a quarter second, so Ctrl-C reaches
  it.
  (9) SharedCurryModule.find handed Python a reference into an entry the
  registry owns weakly: the binding is find_sofilename now and returns a
  copy of the file name, or None.
  (10) The warning of import_ is once per edit of a module object, not
  once per call of import_ from user code: the review asked for once per
  import_.  Every import of a dependent, the expression module of every
  evaluation and the materialization of the Python backend call
  interp.import_ of a loaded name, and nothing tells a user's call from
  those without a keyword threaded through six internal callers (or a new
  keyword of the public import_), which is beyond the smallest change.
  The memo moved from a process-wide dictionary to the module object
  (Handle.warned_mtime), so a second interpreter that loaded the module
  warns on its own; the once-per-call rule is the owner's to confirm.
  The REPL prints it once per :load as decided.
