2026-10-09: set functions, the captured arguments and the failure rule
  (decisions D2 and D1 of the memo on the Fair Scheme proofs; #117;
  worktree wt/setfun)
- The owner approved the memo of 2026-10-08 with its recommendations on
  2026-10-09.  Two of its decisions change the semantics of the set
  functions, and this lane implements them on both backends.  D2 (option
  O1): the arguments the function value given to set_k holds are boxed as
  the value arguments are, so their non-determinism escapes the capsule;
  captureS stays the explicit capture.  D1 (option F2, the amendment
  A-fail): a failure that comes from an argument and is demanded inside
  the capsule fails the set function, behind the flag setfunction_failures,
  whose default stays 'encapsulate' in this lane.  D3, the shared capsule
  (#86), is the next lane: nothing here changes the ownership of a capsule,
  and test_shared_capsule keeps its expected-failure mark.
- D2, where the boxing happens.  setN rewrites set_k f a1 .. ak to evalS
  (applyS (.. (set f) ..) ak); applyS boxed each ai (a SetGuard without a
  set, which evalS gives the new set) and set copied the partial
  application f as it was, so a partial application's arguments and the
  variables a lambda closes over (lambda lifting makes them arguments of
  the lifted function) entered the capsule unboxed: a choice or a free
  variable among them was encapsulated when the enclosing context had not
  decided it, and escaped when it had (choice_escapes, the rule of
  2026-10-05).  Now set_step (currylib/setfunctions.cpp) and set_
  (backends/py/currylib/setfunctions.py) put every argument of the partial
  application under a guard without a set, below which the guards crossed
  on the way to the function value stay (guard_successors); evalS_step
  gives the top guard the new set as before.  This is the entry rule of
  the dissertation (chapter 4, p. 158), which boxes a_0 .. a_n, applied to
  the arguments a partial application holds, which the dissertation treats
  as data (p. 153).  What stays unboxed: an argument applied with captureS
  ($<), an encapsulated expression (set0, exprS), and the head of the
  function value.  The rule of choice_escapes for a captured choice an
  enclosing configuration decided stays for them, and holds_private_state
  stays as it was: a captured choice decided before the capsule starts is
  still found by the walk, through the guard, and the capsule is still a
  private copy; inside it the choice now escapes through the guard and the
  split sorts the one configuration to its side (one split and one restart
  instead of the fork in place that pruned it before).  Both readings give
  the values of the test corpus; the benchmarks below show no counter
  moved.
- D2, a captured variable or choice inside a nested data structure of the
  closure (the decision the task left here): the whole captured argument
  node is boxed, as a value argument is, and the box propagates as it does
  for a value argument.  A pattern match passes the box on to the
  component through rvalue (fst p with p = (x, 2) captured: capData).  An
  application of a partial application reached through guards (a captured
  closure, capPartial; a value argument that is a closure, argPartial)
  copied the arguments of the partial application out of the box, so a
  choice inside them was encapsulated; this held for value arguments
  before this lane (argPartial gave ([0, 1], 0) and ([0, 1], 1), values
  outside the reference semantics, the same defect as #117 one level
  down).  apply_step (prelude/apply.cpp) and apply (prelude/apply.py) now
  put the guards crossed on the way to the partial application on each of
  its arguments, in the completed application and in the extended partial
  application (guard_successors in builtins.cpp, the box rule for a
  partial application as data: a reference to a boxed expression is
  boxed); applyS and captureS do the same for the arguments the partial
  application already holds; and ($!), ($!!) and ($##) build their apply
  node from the rvalues of the function and the argument, which kept the
  guards of neither.  These three follow from the box rule and from D2's
  "box the whole argument"; they are listed under the owner's items below
  because they reach a value argument's closure, beyond the two rules as
  stated.
- D1, what a boxed failure is.  The runtime has one failure node, so the
  box flag of the memo is read from the graph: a failure is boxed when the
  path from the root of the configuration to it crosses a set guard.  The
  C++ runtime reads the path from the levels of the scan (the root to the
  redex; Scan::frames) and from the guards of the inductive variable (the
  redex to the position): failure_escapes and fail_capsule in
  state/rts_setfunctions.cpp, called at the two places a step meets a
  failure inside a capsule, hnf and procN of fairscheme.cpp (a failure at
  the root of a configuration, which procD meets, is unboxed, because a
  guard at the root is a T_SETGRD node that procN descends).  The Python
  backend reads the call stack of the configuration (the guards of the
  variable of each hnf
  frame, and the guards of each walk of N: the guards of its variable,
  kept as base_guards of the WalkState, and the set ids it pushed) and
  escape_all, which D sets when it strips a guard of the current set at
  the root: boxed_failure in eval/rts_setfunctions.py, called in D, N and
  hnf.  Under 'escape' a boxed failure returns the new status E_SETFAIL
  (fwd.hpp; the exception E_SETFAIL on the Python backend), which every
  enclosing step hands out as it hands E_TERMINATE out, the nested procD
  yields it, and allValues_step (allValues) rewrites the redex of the set
  function to a failure under the guards of the enclosing sets the
  failure crossed, less the current set (capsule_failure_guards): so an
  enclosing set function whose argument the failure came from fails in
  turn when it demands it (nestFail), and a failure made inside the outer
  function and passed to an inner set function fails the inner one and
  drops the alternative of the outer function (nestFailInner).  The queue
  of the failed capsule is left as it is; nothing runs it again.  Under
  'encapsulate' (the default) every failure drops its alternative as
  before (rule SF.5).  A value the program consumed before the failing
  argument is demanded stays a value under both settings (lazyFirst): the
  set is lazy, as the values of SF.3 are.  The flag is threaded as
  setfunction_strategy is: FLAG_INFO and the docstring of
  interpreter/flags.py, the table of the Python API page, SetFFailures in
  fwd.hpp, a field and a constructor argument of RuntimeState, the enum of
  the bindings (constants.cpp) and the mapping of cyrtbindings/__init__.py;
  the Python backend reads it as rts.setfunction_failures.
  unit_cxx_rotation.py constructs RuntimeStateBase directly and gained the
  argument.
