- Review of 2026-10-09 (three findings, applied in this worktree).  (i)
  Under the Memory Pool System back end (make GC=mps) the map of the walk
  held the addresses of the bindings across the allocations of evalS_step
  (the goal, the guards) before the new queue took it, and the map is no
  root: a node moved there left a forwarding object in the map.  Not
  observed (the installs use the default collector, which collects at
  the safepoint alone); evalS_step holds a GcClamp from the walk to the
  swap, the rule of gc/mps.cpp for a holder of node addresses across
  allocations.  (ii) A binding that predates the capsule, made by the
  outermost configuration while alone in its queue, was not absorbed at
  the creation (the shortcut skipped the walk), so after a later fork on
  something else each alternative alive at its first read cloned the
  capsule and redid the nested work: boundThenFork, values 1 and 11, went
  from 32 to 40 steps on the C++ backend against the base install (30 to
  33 on the Python backend), with one clone.  Now the walk runs for such a
  configuration when it holds a binding, to absorb alone (no private copy;
  a configuration without a binding still skips it): boundThenFork is back
  to 32/2 and 30/2 with no clone, and every other shape measured is
  unchanged.  (iii) One alternative that reaches the shared capsule
  through two references clones it twice, once per reference, since the
  clone goes into a private copy of the spine of the reference that read
  the binding and the shared node stays for the other: twoRefs gives
  (0, 11) and (0, 22), right, at 63 steps on the C++ backend against 46
  for plainTwoRefs (Python 45 against 24), two clones.  The remedy is the
  per-configuration map from a copied node to its copy that the comments
  of #86 name, the owner's design decision; the shape is pinned with its
  values and its clone count (item 9 below).  Tests: test_clone_per_reader
  (one clone per shared shape of section 5, none for privateCase),
  test_binding_before_fork (no clone) and test_two_references (two), with
  the clone count checked on the Python backend by a child that counts
  clone_queue (the C++ runtime has no counter of the clones); the file has
  16 tests, which pass on both backends and in the stress mode of the
