2026-10-09: three findings of the differential harness (#119, #120, #121;
  worktree wt/bugs3)
- The programs.  The harness of the Fair Scheme proofs generated the three
  programs on 2026-10-09 and the owner filed them.  Each one is a test
  module now: data/curry/SharedListPrinter.curry (#119, pinned by
  unit_show.py), data/curry/PyGeneratorAssert.curry (#120) and
  data/curry/PyFingerprintNone.curry (#121, the module Prog of the harness
  under the name of the file), both pinned by TestHarnessFindings of
  unit_setfunctions_bugs.py on both backends.
- #119, the C++ printer wrote "..." for a shared list without a cycle.
  The cause: StrStringifier (graph/show.cpp) kept every node met in a
  multiset and took a second count for a cycle.  The exit callback of the
  walk removed walk->cursor(), which is the last successor visited at the
  level the walk leaves, not the node left, so a node stayed in the
  multiset after the walk left it, and its next occurrence printed as an
  ellipsis: ([False], ...) for let d = [False] in (d, d), [[1], ...] for
  let d = [1] in d : d.  The fix: the printer keeps the path from the root
  to the current node (a vector, with a multiset for the lookup).  A node
  enters the path when the printer extends the walk below it (the new
  member extend, which every extension of StrStringifier goes through) and
  leaves it in the callback (the last callback, for the root level of the
  walk, closes no extension).  A node met again while it is on the path is
  a cycle; a node met again elsewhere is shared and prints in full.
  is_terminus went: a node the walk never extends is never on the path.
  ReprStringifier was right already (its callback data is the node).  The
  Python printer (show.py) had a leak of its own: format_as_list counted a
  spine node that closed a cycle and never took the count back, so a
  cyclic list shared by two positions printed as ([1, ...], ...) for
  let a = 1:a in (a, a).  Fixed: the failed push is popped.  Both backends
  print ([1, ...], [1, ...]) now, and every cyclic text observed before the
  change stays: [1, ...], [1, 2, ...], Just ..., (..., 1), [...], ... .
  The review found a second limit of the Python printer: the string style
  (format_as_string) walked the spine of a list of Chars with no check for
  a cycle, so str of let s = 'a':s in s never ended on the Python backend,
  where the C++ printer writes ['a', ...].  Fixed: the walk keeps the nodes
  it met and gives up at the first repeat; the list style then writes the
  ellipsis.  Both backends print ['a', ...] and (['a', ...], ['a', ...]).
  Tests: unit_show.py (14 tests, both backends): the two goals of the
  issue evaluated, seven shared shapes (tuple, constructor, string, list in
  list, open list, head and tail), and the cyclic shapes with their texts,
  the shared cyclic list and the cyclic string among them.  test_circular
  of unit_expr.py holds.
- #120, the Python backend asserted in get_generator when sortValues
  compared values with free variables.  The cause: the graph copier that
  makes a value (rts_control.make_value, also the values of a set function
  inside allValues) copied the node of a free variable, so the value held
  a second node with the id of the variable.  The comparison narrowed the
  copy: hnf made the generator on the copy (instantiate rewrites the node
  at hand), and the next hnf of the same copy found the generator on it
  but read the node of the id from the table (get_generator goes through
  vtable), where the original had none; the assert followed the vain
  constrain_equal of the original with itself.  The C++ copier shares a
  free variable ("one node stands for one id", graph/copy.cpp), so the C++
  backend never had two nodes.  The fix: the Python copier shares a free
  variable too and counts it as before (external_freevars).  The comments
  of make_value, InterpreterState and GraphCopier say so.  The expected
  values: the issue records four values of the C++ backend at 1b7ae501.
  The C++ backend at 7eeac2e9 (this base) prints six, the product of the
  three values of f3 (Just True) (S (S Z) twice, Z) and the two sides of
  x15, the free variable of the argument that the capsule returns among
  its values and that sortValues narrows outside; the runs.jsonl of
  campaign 1 records the same six for KiCS2 and for every C++ mode.  The
  four were a loss of the shared capsule before #86 (two of the three
  alternatives of the first component lost x15 = True).  The test pins the
  six on both backends.  Tests: test_free_variable_in_values
  (unit_setfunctions_bugs.py, both backends) and
  test_free_variable_is_shared (unit_py_copy.py).
- #121, the Python backend raised TypeError in read_fp with the id None.
  The cause: the step of =:= (constr_eq in currylib/prelude/constr.py)
  built the pair of a strict constraint with rts.expr((lhs, rhs)) from the
  two Variables, that is from their rvalues, which carry the set guards
  crossed on the way to the target.  In the program the right side, the
  variable x19 of the goal, is reached through the box of a captured
  argument (#117), so the pair held SetGuard(sid, x19), and obj_id of the
  guard is None: D passed it to constrain_equal, equate_fp, update_fp and
  read_fp.  The C++ step builds pair(lhs.target, rhs.target), the two
  variables themselves.  The fix: the Python step does the same for the
  strict constraint.  Test: test_constraint_through_a_guard
  (unit_setfunctions_bugs.py, both backends): (_a, _b, []) and
  (_a, _b, [False]) twice, the values of the C++ backend; the record of
  campaign 1 shows KiCS2 with seven values, x19 instantiated, a difference
  of the kind of row 2 of the memo and not the acceptance.  The review
  found the same cause in make_value_bindings (eval/rts_bindings.py): the
  pair of the value binding of a variable of a builtin type took the
  Variable that hnf passes, that is its rvalue with the guards, so for a
  variable of the goal reached through the box of a captured argument the
  pair held SetGuard(sid, y).  constrain_equal read the id None from both
  sides, took the sides for equal and returned True without a binding, and
  the comparison that followed suspended: let y free in
  (y, sortValues (set1 k2 y)) with k2 m = (m =:= 1) &> (m == 1) printed
  "Evaluation Suspended" on the Python backend, where the C++ backend
  prints (_a, [True]) (the binding is private to the capsule).  Fixed: the
  pair holds the node of the variable, as the C++ step does.  Test:
  test_value_binding_through_a_guard (unit_setfunctions_bugs.py, both
  backends) on the goal valueBinding of PyFingerprintNone.curry.
